window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-cbse12-acc-partnership-basics","kind":"practice","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"title":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts · Practice","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","subject":"Accountancy","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-001","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":1,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Under the fluctuating capital method, a partner's capital account may sometimes show a debit balance.\nReason (R): Under the fluctuating capital method, drawings, interest on drawings and share of loss are recorded directly in the partner's capital account.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT notes that under the fluctuating method the capital account may sometimes show a debit balance. Under the fixed method, by contrast, the capital account always shows a credit balance.\nR is true. Only one account is kept for each partner, so all adjustments are posted directly to it.\nR explains A. If heavy drawings, interest on drawings or a share of loss are debited and they exceed the credits, the single capital account turns debit.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-002","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":2,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"X, Y and Z are partners sharing profits in the ratio 2:2:1. On dissolution, the firm owes its creditors Rs. 9,00,000, but the firm's assets realise only Rs. 3,00,000. X and Y have no private assets, while Z owns private property worth Rs. 10,00,000. What is the maximum amount the creditors can recover from Z's private assets?","image":"","options":["Rs. 1,20,000","Rs. 2,00,000","Rs. 6,00,000","Rs. 9,00,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: debts Rs. 9,00,000 and firm's assets Rs. 3,00,000.\nUnpaid balance = 9,00,000 − 3,00,000 = Rs. 6,00,000.\nEach partner is liable jointly with the others and also severally, and the liability is unlimited.\nSo creditors can recover the entire unpaid balance from any one partner's private assets.\nZ's property (Rs. 10,00,000) covers the full balance, so creditors can recover Rs. 6,00,000. His profit share of 1/5 (Rs. 1,20,000) does not limit this.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-003","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":3,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Priya, Qadir and Rohan have fixed capitals of Rs. 1,00,000, Rs. 60,000 and Rs. 40,000 respectively. Interest on capital @ 10% p.a., provided in the deed, was omitted for two years. Their profit sharing ratio was 2:1:1 in the first year and 1:2:1 in the second year. The error is rectified by one adjusting entry. By what amount will Priya's capital account be credited?","image":"","options":["Rs. 5,000","Rs. 20,000","Rs. 2,500","Rs. 15,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: interest per year = Priya 10,000, Qadir 6,000, Rohan 4,000 (total 20,000). Over 2 years this is 20,000, 12,000 and 8,000.\nYear 1 reduction of 20,000 at 2:1:1 = 10,000, 5,000, 5,000.\nYear 2 reduction of 20,000 at 1:2:1 = 5,000, 10,000, 5,000.\nTotal debits = Priya 15,000, Qadir 15,000, Rohan 10,000.\nNet effect:\n- Priya: 20,000 − 15,000 = Cr 5,000\n- Qadir: Dr 3,000\n- Rohan: Dr 2,000\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-004","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":4,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"X and Y share profits in the ratio 3:2. They admit Z for a 1/5 share of profits. X and Y continue to share the remaining profit in their old ratio. X alone guarantees that Z's share will not be less than Rs. 25,000. The firm's profit for the year is Rs. 1,00,000. What is X's final share of profit?","image":"","options":["Rs. 48,000","Rs. 45,000","Rs. 43,000","Rs. 57,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: Z's share is 1/5, so 4/5 remains for X and Y in the ratio 3:2.\nNew ratio: X = 4/5 × 3/5 = 12/25, Y = 4/5 × 2/5 = 8/25, Z = 5/25. The ratio is 12:8:5.\nShares: X = 12/25 × 1,00,000 = Rs. 48,000, Y = Rs. 32,000, Z = Rs. 20,000.\nDeficiency for Z = 25,000 − 20,000 = Rs. 5,000, borne by X alone.\nSo X's final share = 48,000 − 5,000 = Rs. 43,000.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-005","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":5,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Ira and Jay share profits equally. Their capitals are Rs. 2,40,000 and Rs. 1,60,000. The deed allows interest on capital at 10% p.a. The firm's profit for the year is only Rs. 25,000. The amount credited to Ira as interest on capital will be:","image":"","options":["Rs. 24,000","Rs. 12,500","Rs. 15,000","Rs. 10,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Interest due: Ira $2,40,000 \\times 10\\% = 24,000$ and Jay $1,60,000 \\times 10\\% = 16,000$. Total due = Rs. 40,000.\nAvailable profit is Rs. 25,000, which is less than the interest due. So interest is restricted to the profit.\nThe profit is then shared in the ratio of interest due, 24,000 : 16,000 = 3:2.\nIra gets $25,000 \\times \\frac{3}{5}$ = Rs. 15,000.\nRs. 12,500 wrongly uses the equal profit sharing ratio.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-006","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":6,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Pooja, Qasim and Ravi share profits in the ratio 2:2:1. Their drawings for the year were Pooja Rs. 60,000, Qasim Rs. 20,000 and Ravi Rs. 20,000, withdrawn evenly throughout the year. The deed provides interest on drawings @ 10% p.a., but this was ignored when the final accounts were prepared. Which single adjusting entry rectifies the error?","image":"","options":["Pooja's Capital A/c Dr. 1,000\n  To Qasim's Capital A/c 1,000","Qasim's Capital A/c Dr. 1,000\n  To Pooja's Capital A/c 1,000","Pooja's Capital A/c Dr. 3,000\n  To Qasim's Capital A/c 1,000\n  To Ravi's Capital A/c 1,000\n  To Profit and Loss Adjustment A/c 1,000","Pooja's Capital A/c Dr. 1,000\n  To Ravi's Capital A/c 1,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: drawings are withdrawn evenly, so the average period is 6 months.\nInterest on drawings = Pooja Rs. 3,000, Qasim Rs. 1,000, Ravi Rs. 1,000; total Rs. 5,000.\nThis Rs. 5,000 would have added to profit. Shared 2:2:1, it gives Pooja Rs. 2,000, Qasim Rs. 2,000 and Ravi Rs. 1,000 (credit).\nNet effect:\n- Pooja: 3,000 Dr − 2,000 Cr = Dr 1,000\n- Qasim: 1,000 Dr − 2,000 Cr = Cr 1,000\n- Ravi: nil\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-007","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":7,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (situation) with List-II (treatment of the deficiency of a guaranteed partner).\nList-I:\n(a) Guarantee given by all old partners, with no special ratio agreed\n(b) Guarantee given personally by only one old partner\n(c) Old partners agree to bear the deficiency in a ratio different from their profit sharing ratio\n(d) Guaranteed partner's share at the profit sharing ratio exceeds the guaranteed amount\nList-II:\n(i) Entire deficiency is borne by that partner alone\n(ii) No deficiency arises; the partner gets his actual share\n(iii) Borne by the old partners in their profit sharing ratio\n(iv) Borne by the old partners in the agreed ratio","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a)-(iii): When all old partners give the guarantee, the deficiency is usually shared in their profit sharing ratio, as with Madhulika and Rakshita sharing 2:3.\n(b)-(i): When only one partner gives the guarantee, he bears the whole deficiency, as John did for Mohanty.\n(c)-(iv): When a different ratio is agreed, that ratio is used, as Mahesh and Dinesh did with 3:2.\n(d)-(ii): If the actual share is more than the guarantee, nothing has to be made good.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-008","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":8,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Kiran and Leela are partners with no partnership deed. On October 1, 2019, Kiran advanced a loan of Rs. 2,00,000 to the firm. Kiran demands interest at 10% p.a. on the loan. What amount of interest on loan should be allowed to Kiran for the year ending March 31, 2020?","image":"","options":["Rs. 6,000","Rs. 10,000","Rs. 12,000","Rs. 3,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Loan = Rs. 2,00,000, outstanding from October 1 to March 31, which is 6 months. There is no deed.\nRule: When the deed is silent, the Partnership Act allows interest on a partner's loan at 6% p.a. Kiran's 10% claim is not valid.\nSubstituting: $2,00,000 \\times \\frac{6}{100} \\times \\frac{6}{12}$ = Rs. 6,000.\nRs. 12,000 would wrongly charge interest for the full year.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-009","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":9,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Pallavi and Qadir are partners. Their capitals on April 1, 2023 were Rs. 3,00,000 and Rs. 1,00,000. On July 1, 2023, they agreed that each capital should be Rs. 2,00,000. The necessary adjustments were made by withdrawing or introducing cash. Interest on capital is allowed at 8% p.a., and books are closed on March 31. What is the interest on Qadir's capital for 2023-24?","image":"","options":["Rs. 16,000","Rs. 8,000","Rs. 14,000","Rs. 12,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: Qadir had Rs. 1,00,000 from April 1 to June 30 (3 months). He then brought in Rs. 1,00,000, so he had Rs. 2,00,000 from July 1 to March 31 (9 months).\nFormula: Interest = Capital × 8/100 × months/12 for each period.\nSubstituting:\n- 1,00,000 × 8/100 × 3/12 = Rs. 2,000\n- 2,00,000 × 8/100 × 9/12 = Rs. 12,000\nSo total interest = Rs. 14,000.\nOption (1) wrongly applies the new capital for the whole year.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-010","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":10,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"According to Section 4 of the Indian Partnership Act 1932, partnership is the 'relation between persons who have agreed to share the profits of a business carried on by ______'. Choose the correct words to fill the blank.","image":"","options":["all of them jointly, and no one else","all or any of them acting for all","any one of them acting for himself","a manager appointed by all of them"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Section 4 defines partnership as the relation between persons who have agreed to share the profits of a business carried on by all or any of them acting for all.\nThe words 'acting for all' create mutual agency. Each partner is both a principal and an agent of the other partners.\nOption (1) is wrong because the business need not be run by all partners together.\nOption (3) is wrong because a partner who acts only for himself is not acting for the firm.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-011","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":11,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Pari and Quresh share profits in the ratio 3 : 2. Their capitals are Rs. 2,00,000 and Rs. 1,00,000. The deed allows interest on capital at 10% p.a. and treats it as an appropriation of profit. The firm's net profit for the year is only Rs. 18,000. How much interest on capital will be credited to Pari?","image":"","options":["Rs. 12,000","Rs. 20,000","Rs. 10,800","Rs. 9,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Interest due: Pari = 10% × 2,00,000 = Rs. 20,000; Quresh = 10% × 1,00,000 = Rs. 10,000; total Rs. 30,000. \nThe profit of Rs. 18,000 is less than the interest due. Since interest on capital is an appropriation, it is restricted to the available profit. \nThe profit is then shared in the ratio of interest on capital, 20,000 : 10,000 = 2 : 1. \nPari = $\\frac{2}{3}$ × 18,000 = Rs. 12,000. \nRs. 10,800 wrongly uses the profit sharing ratio 3 : 2. \nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-012","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":12,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): It is desirable that partners put their partnership agreement in writing.\nReason (R): A written deed clearly records matters such as capital contribution, profit sharing ratio and interest rates, which helps avoid disputes among partners.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: although an oral agreement is valid, a written agreement is preferred.\nR is true: the deed sets out the terms on capital, profit sharing ratio, interest, salary and similar matters.\nThe clarity of these written terms is exactly why a written deed reduces disputes, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-013","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":13,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Kiran is a partner in a firm whose books are closed on December 31 every year. The deed provides interest on drawings at 8% p.a. During 2023 she withdrew:\nMarch 1: Rs. 12,000\nJune 1: Rs. 9,000\nSeptember 1: Rs. 15,000\nNovember 30: Rs. 6,000\nAn accountant suggested simply charging interest on the total for 6 months. What is the correct interest on Kiran's drawings?","image":"","options":["Rs. 1,680","Rs. 2,075","Rs. 3,360","Rs. 1,660"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The dates are known, so the product method applies, not the 6-month shortcut.\nMonths to December 31: 10, 7, 4 and 1.\nProducts: $1{,}20{,}000 + 63{,}000 + 60{,}000 + 6{,}000 = 2{,}49{,}000$\nInterest = $2{,}49{,}000 \\times \\frac{8}{100} \\times \\frac{1}{12} = 1{,}660$\nRs. 1,680 is the accountant's shortcut: Rs. 42,000 × 8% × 6/12. Rs. 3,360 charges a full year on Rs. 42,000.\nSo interest = Rs. 1,660.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-014","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":14,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Kirti, a partner, withdrew Rs. 4,000 in the middle of every month during the year ended March 31, 2024. Interest on drawings is charged at 9% p.a. What is the interest on her drawings?","image":"","options":["Rs. 2,160","Rs. 2,340","Rs. 1,980","Rs. 4,320"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Rs. 4,000 per month in the middle of each month, rate 9% p.a.\nTotal drawings = 4,000 × 12 = Rs. 48,000.\nFor mid-month drawings the average period is 6 months, since (11.5 + 0.5)/2 = 6.\nInterest = 48,000 × 9/100 × 6/12 = Rs. 2,160.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-015","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":15,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Arjun, Bhavya and Chetan share profits in the ratio 2 : 2 : 1. Their capitals are Rs. 3,00,000, Rs. 2,00,000 and Rs. 1,00,000, and interest on capital is allowed at 10% p.a. Arjun personally guarantees that Chetan's share of profit, after charging interest on capital, will not be less than Rs. 35,000. The profit before interest on capital is Rs. 2,00,000. What is the total amount credited to Chetan for the year, including interest on his capital?","image":"","options":["Rs. 35,000","Rs. 28,000","Rs. 45,000","Rs. 38,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Interest on capital (10%): Arjun 30,000, Bhavya 20,000, Chetan 10,000, a total of 60,000.\nProfit left for sharing = 2,00,000 − 60,000 = 1,40,000.\nChetan's share = $\\frac{1}{5}$ × 1,40,000 = 28,000. The deficiency of 7,000 is borne by Arjun alone.\nThe guarantee applies to the share of profit after interest. Chetan therefore receives 35,000 as profit plus 10,000 as interest = Rs. 45,000.\nRs. 35,000 leaves out the interest on capital.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-016","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":16,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following best describes the Profit and Loss Appropriation Account of a partnership firm?","image":"","options":["A substitute for the Profit and Loss Account that is used to find the net profit of the firm","An extension of the Profit and Loss Account that starts with net profit or loss and shows how it is distributed among the partners","A personal account of each partner that records drawings and fresh capital","An account prepared only when the firm suffers a net loss"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT describes the Profit and Loss Appropriation Account as merely an extension of the Profit and Loss Account. \nIt begins with the net profit or net loss shown by the Profit and Loss Account. \nIt then records interest on capital, salary, commission and interest on drawings, and finally shows the share of profit or loss of each partner. \nOption (1) fails because net profit is worked out in the Profit and Loss Account, not in this account. \nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-017","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":17,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Under the fixed capital method, a partner's capital account balance changes when the partner permanently withdraws a part of the capital as agreed among the partners.\nStatement II: Under the fluctuating capital method, a partner's capital account can never show a debit balance.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. A fixed capital stays the same only until additional capital is introduced or a part of capital is permanently withdrawn.\nStatement II is incorrect. Under the fluctuating method, drawings and losses are debited directly to the capital account, so it may sometimes show a debit balance.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-018","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":18,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"P, Q and R are partners. Their written partnership deed fixes the profit sharing ratio at 2:2:1. P and Q, who together hold a majority, pass a resolution changing the ratio to 3:2:1 from the current year. R does not agree to the change. Which of the following correctly describes the position?","image":"","options":["The change is valid because P and Q form a majority of the partners.","The change is invalid; profits will continue to be shared in the ratio 2:2:1 as per the existing deed.","The change becomes valid once the revised deed is registered with the Registrar of Firms.","The change is invalid, so the deed is treated as silent and profits will be shared equally under the Partnership Act."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A clause of the deed can be altered only with the consent of all partners. Without R's consent, the 3:2:1 ratio does not take effect.\nThe existing clause (2:2:1) stays in force, so profits continue in that ratio.\nOption (4) is tempting, but it is wrong. The deed is not silent on the ratio, so the equal-sharing rule of the Act does not apply.\nRegistration cannot validate a change that lacks the consent of all partners.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-019","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":19,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"When a partner brings in additional capital during the accounting year, interest on that additional capital is calculated for the period:","image":"","options":["from the first day of the accounting year to the date of introduction","for the full accounting year, whatever the date of introduction","for an average period of six months","from the date of introduction to the last day of the accounting year"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Interest on capital is time-based. It is allowed only for the period the money actually remained in the business.\nAdditional capital is used by the firm only after it is introduced. So interest runs from the date of introduction to the last day of the financial year.\nA full year is wrong because the money was not with the firm before that date.\nThe six-month average period applies to drawings withdrawn evenly, not to capital.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-020","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":20,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Under the fixed capital method, the current account of partner Ravi shows a debit balance of ₹18,000 at the end of the year. How will this balance appear in the firm's Balance Sheet?","image":"","options":["On the liabilities side, added to Ravi's capital","On the assets side","On the liabilities side, deducted from Ravi's capital","It is not shown in the Balance Sheet; it is transferred to the Profit and Loss Appropriation Account"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Under the fixed capital method, each partner has two accounts: a capital account and a current account.\nThe capital account always has a credit balance and is shown on the liabilities side.\nThe current account can have a debit or a credit balance. A credit balance goes on the liabilities side. A debit balance goes on the assets side.\nOption (3) is tempting, but deducting from capital only happens when there is a single fluctuating capital account. Here the current account is a separate account.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-021","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":21,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"The deed of Neha and Om allows Om a commission of 10% of the net profit after charging such commission. The net profit before this commission is Rs. 1,32,000. What is Om's commission?","image":"","options":["Rs. 13,200","Rs. 10,800","Rs. 11,000","Rs. 12,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: profit before commission = Rs. 1,32,000; commission = 10% of profit after commission. \nIf profit after commission is 100, the commission is 10, so profit before commission is 110. \nCommission = 1,32,000 × $\\frac{10}{110}$ = Rs. 12,000. \nCheck: 1,32,000 − 12,000 = 1,20,000, and 10% of 1,20,000 = 12,000. \nRs. 13,200 is 10% of the profit before commission, which ignores the 'after charging' condition. \nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-022","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":22,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, what is the main purpose of a partnership deed providing for interest on drawings?","image":"","options":["To discourage partners from withdrawing excessive amounts from the firm","To increase the capital balances of partners who withdraw more","To reduce the firm's net profit as shown by the Profit and Loss Account","To reward partners who withdraw money regularly"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT states that charging interest on drawings discourages excessive drawings by the partners.\nThe interest is debited to the partners' capital or current accounts, so it reduces their balances. It does not increase them.\nIt is credited to the Profit and Loss Appropriation Account, not charged against profit in the Profit and Loss Account. So option (3) fails.\nIt penalises withdrawals rather than rewarding them.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-023","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":23,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Nikhil and Ojas formed a partnership with no partnership deed. How many of the following claims are valid under the Indian Partnership Act, 1932?\n(i) Nikhil wants interest at 8% p.a. on the loan he gave the firm.\n(ii) Ojas, who contributed less capital, wants an equal share of profits.\n(iii) Nikhil wants interest on capital at 6% p.a.\n(iv) Ojas wants interest charged on Nikhil's drawings.\n(v) Nikhil wants interest at 6% p.a. on his loan to the firm.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Invalid: interest on a partner's loan is limited to 6% p.a.\n(ii) Valid: profits are shared equally, whatever the capital contributed.\n(iii) Invalid: no interest on capital is allowed without an agreement.\n(iv) Invalid: no interest is charged on drawings without an agreement.\n(v) Valid: the Act allows 6% p.a. interest on a partner's loan.\nTwo claims are valid.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-024","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":24,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Arjun and Bela share profits in the ratio 1:1. Their capitals are Rs. 2,40,000 and Rs. 1,60,000. The deed allows interest on capital at 10% p.a. The firm's net profit for the year is Rs. 30,000. How much will be credited to Arjun's capital account as interest on capital?","image":"","options":["Rs. 24,000","Rs. 18,000","Rs. 15,000","Rs. 12,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: interest due is Arjun Rs. 24,000 and Bela Rs. 16,000, a total of Rs. 40,000. Profit is only Rs. 30,000.\nRule: when profit is less than the interest due, interest is restricted to the available profit. The profit is shared in the ratio of interest on capital, 24,000 : 16,000 = 3:2.\nSubstituting: Arjun's share = 30,000 × 3/5 = 18,000.\nSo Arjun is credited Rs. 18,000, not the full Rs. 24,000.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-025","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":25,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Read the two statements about showing a guarantee of profit in the Profit and Loss Appropriation Account.\nStatement I: The deficiency is deducted from the shares of the guaranteeing partners and added to the share of the guaranteed partner, all on the debit side of the account.\nStatement II: Making good the deficiency increases the total amount of profit credited to all the partners taken together.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is true. In the Mohit–Rohan–Rahul illustration, Mohit's and Rohan's shares are shown less their share of the deficiency. Rahul's share is shown plus the deficiency received. All of this appears on the debit side.\nStatement II is false. The deficiency only moves profit from the guaranteeing partners to the guaranteed partner. The total distributed still equals the net profit, for example Rs. 1,60,000 in that illustration.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-026","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":26,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): If the element of mutual agency is absent, there is no partnership even if persons agree to share profits.\nReason (R): Each partner carrying on the business of the firm is a principal as well as an agent for all the other partners.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT says mutual agency is so important that there would be no partnership without it.\nR is true: each partner can bind the others by his acts and is bound by their acts. This makes him both a principal and an agent.\nR explains A: this two-way agency is exactly what mutual agency means, so it is the test of a partnership.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-027","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":27,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Read the two statements about a partnership agreement.\nStatement I: A partnership is valid only if the agreement between the partners is in writing.\nStatement II: A partnership deed should be drafted as per the Stamp Act and should preferably be registered with the Registrar of Firms.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. The Partnership Act does not require the agreement to be in writing, and an oral agreement is equally valid. A written deed is only preferred because it helps avoid disputes.\nStatement II is correct. NCERT states that the deed should be properly prepared as per the Stamp Act and preferably registered with the Registrar of Firms.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-028","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":28,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Read the following statements about the formation of a partnership.\nStatement I: A partnership comes into existence only when the partners sign a written partnership deed.\nStatement II: An oral agreement between partners is equally valid, although a written deed is preferred to avoid disputes.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct and Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect and Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. The Indian Partnership Act 1932 does not require the partnership agreement to be in writing.\nStatement II is correct. An oral agreement is equally valid. A written deed is preferred only because it helps avoid disputes between partners.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-029","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":29,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (item) with List-II (accounting treatment):\nList-I\n(A) Debit balance of a partner's current account under the fixed capital method\n(B) Interest on a partner's loan to the firm\n(C) Salary allowed to a partner as per the deed\n(D) Interest charged on partners' drawings\nList-II\n(I) Credited to Profit and Loss Appropriation Account\n(II) Shown on the assets side of the Balance Sheet\n(III) Debited to Profit and Loss Account as a charge\n(IV) Debited to Profit and Loss Appropriation Account","image":"","options":["A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A)-(II): a current account with a debit balance is shown on the assets side of the Balance Sheet.\n(B)-(III): interest on a partner's loan is a charge against profit. It is debited to the P&L Account, as in Illustration 4 where Amitabh's loan interest of Rs. 3,000 is charged.\n(C)-(IV): partner's salary is an appropriation of profit, so it is debited to the P&L Appropriation Account.\n(D)-(I): interest on drawings is a gain to the firm and is credited to the P&L Appropriation Account.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-030","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":30,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Ishaan and Yuvi began a partnership on April 1, 2019 without any agreement. Ishaan contributed capital of Rs. 5,00,000 and Yuvi Rs. 2,00,000. On August 1, 2019, Ishaan also advanced a loan of Rs. 2,40,000 to the firm. Ishaan was ill for four months, and Yuvi ran the business alone during that time. The profit for the year ended March 31, 2020, before any interest on the loan, was Rs. 1,10,000.\nIshaan claims interest at 10% p.a. on his capital and his loan, and wants profits shared in the capital ratio. Yuvi claims a salary of Rs. 3,000 per month for the four months.\nSettling the dispute as per the Act, what total amount will Ishaan receive from the firm's profit for the year (interest on loan plus share of profit)?","image":"","options":["Rs. 59,800","Rs. 50,200","Rs. 63,000","Rs. 71,714"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Judging the claims: there is no interest on capital and no salary to Yuvi. Profits are shared equally, and the loan earns 6% p.a.\nThe loan was outstanding for 8 months (Aug 1 to Mar 31). Interest = 2,40,000 × 6/100 × 8/12 = Rs. 9,600.\nDivisible profit = 1,10,000 − 9,600 = Rs. 1,00,400. Ishaan's share = Rs. 50,200.\nIshaan's total = 50,200 + 9,600 = Rs. 59,800.\nRs. 63,000 wrongly uses 10% on the loan. Rs. 71,714 wrongly uses the capital ratio.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-031","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":31,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Rohit and Sachin, who jointly buy a plot of land and hold it as co-owners, become partners.\nStatement II: A partnership is valid only if the partnership deed is registered with the Registrar of Firms.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect: mere co-ownership of property is not partnership. They would be partners only if they carried on a business, such as buying and selling land for profit.\nStatement II is incorrect: the agreement may even be oral. The deed is only 'preferably' registered, so registration is not a condition of validity.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-032","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":32,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Rekha, a partner, made the following drawings during the year ending March 31, 2024:\nMay 31, 2023: Rs. 8,000\nSeptember 30, 2023: Rs. 6,000\nDecember 31, 2023: Rs. 10,000\nJanuary 31, 2024: Rs. 4,000\nInterest on drawings is charged at 9% p.a. Using the product method, calculate the interest on her drawings.","image":"","options":["Rs. 1,260","Rs. 1,155","Rs. 1,386","Rs. 1,050"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: Rate = 9% p.a.; books closed on March 31.\nMonths to year-end: 10, 6, 3 and 2.\nProducts: $8{,}000 \\times 10 = 80{,}000$; $6{,}000 \\times 6 = 36{,}000$; $10{,}000 \\times 3 = 30{,}000$; $4{,}000 \\times 2 = 8{,}000$. Total = 1,54,000.\nFormula: Interest = $1{,}54{,}000 \\times \\frac{9}{100} \\times \\frac{1}{12} = 1{,}155$\nRs. 1,260 is wrong: it is the 6-month average applied to Rs. 28,000, which is used only when dates are not given.\nSo interest = Rs. 1,155.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-033","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":33,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Meher's capital account showed a closing balance of Rs. 2,40,000 on March 31, 2020. During the year:\n• her drawings of Rs. 30,000 were debited to the account;\n• her share of profit of Rs. 50,000 was credited;\n• she introduced additional capital of Rs. 40,000 on October 1, 2019.\nCalculate the interest on her capital at 10% p.a. for 2019-20.","image":"","options":["Rs. 20,000","Rs. 24,000","Rs. 22,000","Rs. 18,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Opening capital = 2,40,000 + 30,000 (drawings) − 50,000 (profit) − 40,000 (additional capital) = Rs. 1,80,000.\nInterest on opening capital for 12 months = $1,80,000 \\times \\frac{10}{100}$ = Rs. 18,000.\nInterest on the addition for 6 months = $40,000 \\times \\frac{10}{100} \\times \\frac{6}{12}$ = Rs. 2,000.\nTotal = Rs. 20,000.\nRs. 24,000 wrongly charges interest on the closing balance.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-034","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":34,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\nA. Partner's current account with a debit balance\nB. Partner's capital account under the fixed capital method\nC. Interest on drawings\nD. Interest on capital\nList-II\n(i) Credited to Profit and Loss Appropriation Account\n(ii) Debited to Profit and Loss Appropriation Account\n(iii) Shown on the assets side of the balance sheet\n(iv) Always shows a credit balance","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(iii): a current account with a debit balance is shown on the assets side of the balance sheet.\nB-(iv): under the fixed capital method, capital accounts always show a credit balance.\nC-(i): interest on drawings is a gain to the firm, so it is credited to the P&L Appropriation Account.\nD-(ii): interest on capital is allowed to partners, so it is debited to the P&L Appropriation Account.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-035","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":35,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Interest on additional capital brought in during the year is calculated from the date it was introduced to the last day of the accounting year.\nStatement II: If the firm's profit is less than the total interest on capital due to the partners, the available profit is distributed among the partners in their profit sharing ratio.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Additional capital earns interest only for the period it stays in the business, that is, from the date of introduction to the year end.\nStatement II is incorrect. When profit is insufficient, interest is restricted to the amount of profit. The profit is then effectively shared in the ratio of interest on capital, not in the profit sharing ratio.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-036","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":36,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): If the partnership deed is silent, a partner who has advanced a loan to the firm is entitled to interest on it at 6% per annum.\nReason (R): Interest on a partner's loan is an appropriation of profit and is therefore debited to the Profit and Loss Appropriation Account.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. Under the Indian Partnership Act 1932, interest on a partner's loan is allowed at 6% p.a. when the deed is silent.\nR is false. Interest on a partner's loan is a charge against profit, not an appropriation of profit. It is debited to the Profit and Loss Account. In Illustration 4, Amitabh's loan interest of Rs. 3,000 appears in the P&L Account, not in the Appropriation Account.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-037","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":37,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A partner who looks after the firm's day-to-day work cannot claim a salary if the partnership deed is silent on the matter.\nReason (R): Under the Indian Partnership Act, 1932, no partner is entitled to salary or other remuneration for taking part in the business unless the deed provides for it.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. An active partner has no right to a salary when the deed does not provide for one.\nR is true. The Act denies remuneration for the firm's work unless the deed provides for it.\nR states the exact rule from which A follows, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-038","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":38,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For the same monthly drawings and the same rate, interest on drawings made at the beginning of each month is more than interest on drawings made at the end of each month.\nReason (R): A drawing made at the beginning of a month remains outstanding for one month more than a drawing made at the end of that month.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Interest is charged for 6.5 months at the beginning against 5.5 months at the end, on the same total amount.\nR is true. A beginning-of-month withdrawal stays out of the business one extra month. For example, the April 1 drawing is outstanding for 12 months, while the April 30 drawing is outstanding for 11 months.\nThis extra month for every drawing is exactly why the average period, and so the interest, is larger. R therefore explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-039","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":39,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Neha and Om share profits in the ratio 3:2, with capitals of Rs 2,00,000 and Rs 1,00,000. The deed provides interest on capital at 5% p.a. but is silent on interest on loans.\nOn April 1, Neha advanced a loan of Rs 50,000 to the firm. The firm also uses Om's building as a godown at a rent of Rs 2,000 per month.\nThe profit for the year, before considering the loan interest and the rent, is Rs 1,52,000. What is Neha's share of the divisible profit?","image":"","options":["Rs 67,800","Rs 66,000","Rs 80,400","Rs 64,800"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: Loan Rs 50,000 at 6% (deed silent), rent Rs 2,000 × 12, interest on capital at 5%.\nStep 1 (charges in P&L A/c): Loan interest = 3,000 and rent = 24,000. Net profit = 1,52,000 − 3,000 − 24,000 = 1,25,000.\nStep 2 (appropriation): Interest on capital = 10,000 + 5,000 = 15,000. Divisible profit = 1,25,000 − 15,000 = 1,10,000.\nStep 3: Neha's share = 1,10,000 × $\\frac{3}{5}$ = Rs 66,000.\nRs 80,400 ignores the rent; Rs 64,800 uses 10% interest on the loan.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-040","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":40,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"A firm maintains partners' capital accounts under the fixed capital method. How many of the following items will appear on the CREDIT side of a partner's current account?\n(i) Interest on capital\n(ii) Drawings\n(iii) Salary to partner\n(iv) Interest on drawings\n(v) Commission to partner\n(vi) Share of profit\n(vii) Additional capital introduced\n(viii) Share of loss","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Interest on capital: credit side of the current account.\n(ii) Drawings and (iv) interest on drawings: debit side.\n(iii) Salary and (v) commission: credit side.\n(vi) Share of profit: credit side. (viii) Share of loss: debit side.\n(vii) Additional capital: recorded in the capital account, not the current account.\nCredit items are (i), (iii), (v) and (vi), which makes 4.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-041","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":41,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"The maximum number of partners in a partnership firm, as presently prescribed by the Central Government, is:","image":"","options":["10","20","50","100"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Section 464 of the Companies Act 2013 empowers the Central Government to prescribe the maximum number of partners in a firm.\nThe number so prescribed cannot exceed 100.\nThe Central Government has actually prescribed 50.\nSo 100 is only the ceiling, not the prescribed limit.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-042","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":42,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Meera and Neel share profits in the ratio 3:2. They admit Om for a 1/6 share of profits with a guaranteed minimum of Rs. 30,000. Meera and Neel continue to share the remaining profit between themselves in the ratio 3:2. However, they agree to bear any deficiency on account of Om's guarantee in the ratio 1:3. The firm's profit for the year is Rs. 1,44,000. What is Neel's final share of profit?","image":"","options":["Rs. 45,600","Rs. 43,500","Rs. 48,000","Rs. 42,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: Om's share is 1/6, so 5/6 remains for Meera and Neel in the ratio 3:2.\nNew ratio: Meera = 5/6 × 3/5 = 1/2, Neel = 5/6 × 2/5 = 1/3, Om = 1/6. The ratio is 3:2:1.\nShares: Meera = Rs. 72,000, Neel = Rs. 48,000, Om = Rs. 24,000.\nDeficiency = 30,000 − 24,000 = Rs. 6,000. Neel bears 3/4 × 6,000 = Rs. 4,500.\nNeel's final share = 48,000 − 4,500 = Rs. 43,500. Option (1) wrongly uses the 3:2 profit ratio for the deficiency.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-043","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":43,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Arjun and Bela started a firm on April 1, 2023 without any partnership agreement. They contributed capitals of Rs. 3,00,000 and Rs. 2,00,000 respectively. Arjun also advanced a loan of Rs. 1,50,000 to the firm on August 1, 2023. The profit for the year ended March 31, 2024, before any interest on the loan, was Rs. 1,00,000. A dispute arose:\nArjun claims interest on capital at 8% p.a. and profit sharing in the capital ratio.\nBela claims a salary of Rs. 2,000 per month for running the business while Arjun was ill.\nSettling the dispute as per the Indian Partnership Act 1932, what is Bela's share of profit?","image":"","options":["Rs. 47,000","Rs. 45,500","Rs. 37,600","Rs. 50,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Rules applied: no interest on capital, no salary, equal sharing, and 6% p.a. on the loan.\nLoan interest (a charge against profit) = 1,50,000 × 6/100 × 8/12 = Rs. 6,000. The loan was outstanding from Aug 1 to Mar 31, i.e. 8 months.\nDistributable profit = 1,00,000 − 6,000 = Rs. 94,000.\nBela's share = 94,000 × 1/2 = Rs. 47,000.\nWhy the others fail: Rs. 45,500 charges loan interest for 12 months. Rs. 37,600 uses the capital ratio. Rs. 50,000 ignores loan interest.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-044","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":44,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Arjun and Bela are partners with capitals of Rs. 6,00,000 and Rs. 2,00,000. They share profits equally. Their partnership deed says nothing about interest on capital. Arjun demands interest on capital at 6% p.a. because he contributed more. The firm earned a profit of Rs. 1,20,000. Under the Indian Partnership Act 1932, which of the following is correct?","image":"","options":["Arjun gets interest at 6% p.a., since 6% is the rate the Act fixes","Neither partner gets interest on capital, and the profit of Rs. 1,20,000 is shared equally","Both partners get interest at 6% p.a. on their capitals","The profit is shared in the capital ratio of 3:1 instead of interest"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Rule: interest on capital is not a matter of right. It is allowed only when the deed expressly provides for it.\nHere the deed is silent, so no partner gets interest on capital.\nWith no agreed ratio, profits are shared equally, whatever the capital contribution.\nOption (1) fails because the Act's 6% rate applies to a partner's loan, not to capital.\nOption (4) fails because capital contribution does not decide the ratio when the deed is silent.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-045","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":45,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Neha and Pooja are partners. Neha withdrew Rs. 25,000 on the first day of every quarter, starting April 1, 2023, for the year ended March 31, 2024. Interest on drawings is charged at 12% p.a. What is the interest on Neha's drawings?","image":"","options":["Rs. 4,500","Rs. 6,000","Rs. 6,500","Rs. 7,500"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: Rs. 25,000 at the beginning of each quarter, so total drawings = Rs. 1,00,000; rate 12% p.a.\nThe drawings remain outstanding for 12, 9, 6 and 3 months. Average period = (12 + 9 + 6 + 3)/4 = 7.5 months.\nInterest = 1,00,000 × 12/100 × 7.5/12 = Rs. 7,500.\nRs. 4,500 would wrongly use the end-of-quarter period of 4.5 months.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-046","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":46,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"After the final accounts of a partnership firm have been prepared and profits distributed, the partners find that interest on capital was omitted. They do not want to reopen the old accounts. Besides adjusting the partners' capital accounts directly, which account may be used to rectify the error?","image":"","options":["Profit and Loss Appropriation Account","Profit and Loss Adjustment Account","Revaluation Account","Realisation Account"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: NCERT says past omissions are corrected without altering old accounts. This is done either through a Profit and Loss Adjustment Account or directly in the partners' capital accounts.\nThe Appropriation Account is the most tempting option, but it is used to distribute the current year's profit. It is not used to correct an earlier year after distribution.\nThe Revaluation Account is used on reconstitution of the firm, and the Realisation Account is used on dissolution.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-047","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":47,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"The partnership deed of a firm is silent on the following matters. Match List-I (matter) with List-II (provision of the Indian Partnership Act, 1932 that applies).\nList-I:\n(A) Profit sharing ratio\n(B) Interest on a partner's loan to the firm\n(C) Interest on drawings\n(D) Salary to a working partner\nList-II:\n(1) No interest is charged\n(2) Equal sharing\n(3) Not payable unless the deed provides for it\n(4) 6% per annum","image":"","options":["A-2, B-4, C-1, D-3","A-2, B-1, C-4, D-3","A-3, B-4, C-1, D-2","A-4, B-2, C-3, D-1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A)-(2): profits are shared equally, whatever the capital contributed.\n(B)-(4): a partner's loan carries interest at 6% per annum.\n(C)-(1): no interest is charged on drawings.\n(D)-(3): no salary is paid unless the deed provides for it.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-048","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":48,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Pranav and Qadir started business on April 1, 2019 with capitals of Rs. 3,00,000 and Rs. 1,00,000. They made no partnership agreement. On July 1, 2019, Pranav gave the firm a loan of Rs. 1,00,000. The profit for the year ended March 31, 2020, before charging any interest on the loan, was Rs. 80,000. What is each partner's share of profit?","image":"","options":["Rs. 40,000","Rs. 37,750","Rs. 56,625 (Pranav)","Rs. 37,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: there is no deed, so profits are shared equally, there is no interest on capital, and the loan earns interest at 6% p.a.\nThe loan was outstanding from July 1 to March 31, which is 9 months.\nInterest on loan = 1,00,000 × 6/100 × 9/12 = Rs. 4,500. This is a charge against profit.\nDivisible profit = 80,000 − 4,500 = Rs. 75,500.\nEach partner's share = 75,500 ÷ 2 = Rs. 37,750.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-049","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":49,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Arjun, Bela and Chitra share profits in the ratio 3:2:1. Chitra is guaranteed a minimum profit of Rs. 15,000, and the guarantee is given by Arjun alone. The firm's net profit for the year is Rs. 60,000. What amount of profit will finally be credited to Arjun?","image":"","options":["Rs. 25,000","Rs. 27,000","Rs. 30,000","Rs. 22,500"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Profit = Rs. 60,000 in the ratio 3:2:1.\nShares: Arjun = Rs. 30,000; Bela = Rs. 20,000; Chitra = Rs. 10,000.\nDeficiency to Chitra = 15,000 − 10,000 = Rs. 5,000.\nOnly Arjun gave the guarantee, so he bears the whole deficiency: 30,000 − 5,000 = Rs. 25,000.\nRs. 27,000 wrongly splits the deficiency between Arjun and Bela in the ratio 3:2.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-050","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":50,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Rekha's fixed capital on April 1, 2019 was Rs. 4,00,000. During 2019-20:\n- She introduced additional capital of Rs. 1,00,000.\n- She permanently withdrew Rs. 50,000 of capital as agreed with her partners.\n- Her drawings were Rs. 30,000.\n- Her share of profit was Rs. 40,000.\nWhat is the closing balance of her Capital Account on March 31, 2020?","image":"","options":["Rs. 4,50,000","Rs. 4,60,000","Rs. 5,00,000","Rs. 4,00,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: opening capital Rs. 4,00,000, additional capital Rs. 1,00,000, permanent withdrawal Rs. 50,000.\nUnder the fixed method, drawings and share of profit go to the current account, so they are ignored here.\nCapital = 4,00,000 + 1,00,000 − 50,000.\nSo closing capital = Rs. 4,50,000. (Rs. 4,60,000 wrongly includes profit and drawings.)\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-051","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":51,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (pattern of equal drawings during a 12-month year) with List-II (average period for which interest is charged on the total drawings):\nList-I\n(a) Fixed amount withdrawn at the beginning of each month\n(b) Fixed amount withdrawn at the end of each month\n(c) Fixed amount withdrawn in the middle of each month\n(d) Fixed amount withdrawn at the beginning of each quarter\nList-II\n(i) 6 months\n(ii) 7½ months\n(iii) 6½ months\n(iv) 5½ months","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Beginning of month: $\\frac{12+1}{2}$ = 6½ months, so (iii).\n(b) End of month: $\\frac{11+0}{2}$ = 5½ months, so (iv).\n(c) Middle of month: $\\frac{11.5+0.5}{2}$ = 6 months, so (i).\n(d) Beginning of quarter: $\\frac{12+3}{2}$ = 7½ months, so (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-052","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":52,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (matter on which the partnership deed is silent) with List-II (provision of the Indian Partnership Act, 1932):\nList-I\n(A) Sharing of profits\n(B) Interest on capital\n(C) Loan advanced by a partner\n(D) Profit made by a partner from a competing business\nList-II\n(i) Interest at 6% p.a.\n(ii) Must be paid over to the firm\n(iii) Equally among partners\n(iv) Not allowed","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Profits are shared equally, irrespective of capital: (iii).\n(B) Interest on capital is not allowed without an agreement: (iv).\n(C) A partner's loan earns interest at 6% p.a.: (i).\n(D) Profit from a business of the same nature that competes with the firm must be accounted for and paid to the firm: (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-053","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":53,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): If a new partner's share of profit calculated at the agreed profit sharing ratio is more than the amount guaranteed to him, he gets his actual share and no deficiency arises.\nReason (R): The amount guaranteed to a partner is treated as an expense of the firm and is debited to the Profit and Loss Account.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. A guarantee only fixes a minimum amount. The guaranteeing partners pay a deficiency only when the partner's share at the profit sharing ratio falls below that amount.\nR is false. A guarantee does not change the firm's profit and is not a charge in the Profit and Loss Account. Any deficiency is adjusted out of the guarantors' shares through the Profit and Loss Appropriation Account.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-054","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":54,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are usually included in a partnership deed?\n(i) Accounting period of the firm\n(ii) Rules regarding operation of bank accounts\n(iii) Treatment of loss arising out of insolvency of a partner\n(iv) Method of settlement of disputes among partners\n(v) Mode of appointment of auditor, if any\n(vi) Names and addresses of the firm's debtors","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Included: the accounting period is a usual clause.\n(ii) Included: rules for operating bank accounts are a usual clause.\n(iii) Included: treatment of insolvency loss is a usual clause.\n(iv) Included: the method of settling disputes is a usual clause.\n(v) Included: the mode of the auditor's appointment is a usual clause.\n(vi) Not included: the firm's debtors are a business record, not a term of the partners' relationship.\nSo 5 items are included.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-055","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":55,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When a firm suffers a net loss for the year, no interest on capital or salary is allowed to the partners, even if the deed provides for them.\nReason (R): Interest on capital and partners' salary are appropriations of profit and not charges against profit.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT notes that when a firm suffers a loss, no interest on capital, salary or remuneration is allowed. In the Yadu–Madhu–Vidu illustration, the whole loss is shared in the profit sharing ratio. \nR is true. These items are debited to the P&L Appropriation A/c, so they are distributions of profit and not expenses. \nBecause they are distributions, they can be made only when there is profit to distribute. So R correctly explains A. \nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-056","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":56,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Neha, a partner, withdrew Rs. 20,000 on the first day of every quarter during the year ending March 31, 2020. The deed provides for interest on drawings at 9% p.a. What is the interest on Neha's drawings?","image":"","options":["Rs. 4,500","Rs. 2,700","Rs. 3,600","Rs. 7,200"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Rs. 20,000 is withdrawn at the beginning of each quarter. Total drawings = Rs. 80,000. Rate = 9% p.a.\nFormula: average period = (12 + 3)/2 = 7.5 months, so Interest = Total drawings × Rate × 7.5/12.\nSubstituting: 80,000 × 9/100 × 7.5/12 = 4,500.\nSo interest on drawings = Rs. 4,500. Rs. 2,700 would wrongly use 4.5 months, which is the period for end-of-quarter withdrawals.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-057","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":57,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about guarantee of minimum profit to a partner are correct?\n(i) The guaranteeing partners make good a deficiency only when the partner's share at the profit sharing ratio is less than the guaranteed amount.\n(ii) Where all old partners give the guarantee, they bear the deficiency in the agreed ratio, which usually is their profit sharing ratio.\n(iii) Giving a guarantee increases the total profit of the firm available for distribution.\n(iv) A guarantee can be given only by all the old partners jointly, never by one partner individually.\n(v) Old partners may agree to bear the deficiency in a ratio different from the ratio in which they share profits.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the guarantee is a minimum, so a deficiency exists only when the actual share falls short.\n(ii) Correct: the agreed ratio is usually the profit sharing ratio.\n(iii) Incorrect: total profit stays the same; the guarantors' shares simply transfer to the guaranteed partner.\n(iv) Incorrect: one old partner can give the guarantee individually.\n(v) Correct: Mahesh and Dinesh share profits 2:1 but bear Rakesh's deficiency 3:2.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-058","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":58,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Asha, Bina and Chetan share profits in the ratio 3 : 2 : 1. Their capitals are Rs. 80,000, Rs. 60,000 and Rs. 40,000. The deed provides:\n- interest on capital at 5% p.a.\n- a salary of Rs. 1,500 per month to Asha\n- a commission of Rs. 6,000 to Bina\n\nInterest on drawings is Asha Rs. 600, Bina Rs. 400 and Chetan Rs. 200. Net profit as per the Profit and Loss Account is Rs. 60,000. What is Asha's share of the divisible profit?","image":"","options":["Rs. 13,500","Rs. 14,100","Rs. 17,100","Rs. 23,100"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: net profit Rs. 60,000; interest on drawings = 600 + 400 + 200 = Rs. 1,200. \nDebits: Asha's salary = 1,500 × 12 = Rs. 18,000; Bina's commission = Rs. 6,000; interest on capital = 4,000 + 3,000 + 2,000 = Rs. 9,000. \nDivisible profit = 60,000 + 1,200 − 18,000 − 6,000 − 9,000 = Rs. 28,200. \nAsha's share = $\\frac{3}{6}$ × 28,200 = Rs. 14,100. \nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-059","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":59,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Read the following statements about past adjustments in a partnership firm.\nStatement I: When an omission is found after the final accounts are prepared and profits distributed, the old accounts are not altered. The error is rectified either through a Profit and Loss Adjustment Account or directly in the partners' capital accounts.\nStatement II: When omitted interest on capital is rectified through the Profit and Loss Adjustment Account, the first entry is: Partners' Capital A/cs Dr. To Profit and Loss Adjustment A/c.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT states that past omissions are corrected without altering old accounts. The correction is made through a Profit and Loss Adjustment Account or directly in the capital accounts.\nStatement II is incorrect. Interest on capital is a credit due to the partners, so the first entry is Profit and Loss Adjustment A/c Dr. To Partners' Capital A/cs.\nThe resulting debit balance (loss on adjustment) is then charged to the partners in their profit sharing ratio.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-060","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":60,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Meera and Kabir are partners in a firm with no partnership deed. On 1 April, Kabir gave the firm a loan of Rs. 1,00,000 for business purposes. Nothing was agreed about interest on this loan. At what rate is Kabir entitled to interest on the loan?","image":"","options":["Nil, because there is no agreement","6% per annum","10% per annum","12% per annum"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Under the Indian Partnership Act 1932, a partner who lends money to the firm for business purposes gets interest on the loan at 6% p.a. when the deed is silent.\nOption (1) is tempting because the Act allows no interest on capital without an agreement. A loan is different from capital. The Act itself provides the 6% rate.\nThe 10% and 12% rates apply only if the partners agree to them.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-061","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":61,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (Feature of partnership) with List-II (Situation):\nList-I\nA. Mutual agency\nB. Unlimited liability\nC. Business\nD. Agreement\nList-II\n(i) A partner's personal car is sold to pay the firm's creditors\n(ii) A contract signed by one partner for the firm's business binds all partners\n(iii) Two persons jointly owning a house, without any trading activity, are not partners\n(iv) A verbal understanding to share profits of a shop is valid","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(iv)","A-(i), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(iii)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(ii): Under mutual agency, one partner's act for the business binds all partners.\nB-(i): Unlimited liability means a partner's private assets, such as a car, can pay the firm's debts.\nC-(iii): Mere co-ownership without business is not partnership.\nD-(iv): An oral agreement is equally valid.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-062","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":62,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Rent paid to a partner for using her personal building as the firm's godown is debited to the Profit and Loss Appropriation Account.\nStatement II: Interest on partners' drawings is shown on the credit side of the Profit and Loss Appropriation Account.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. Rent to a partner is a business expense, a charge against profit. It is debited to the Profit and Loss Account before net profit is found. NCERT's note on Reena's rent says it is charged against profits. \nStatement II is correct. Interest on drawings is a gain to the firm and is credited to the P&L Appropriation A/c. \nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-063","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":63,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"A partnership deed is silent about the method of maintaining partners' capital accounts. The accountant should prepare the capital accounts under the:","image":"","options":["Fixed capital method","Fluctuating capital method","Method chosen by the senior-most partner","Fixed capital method, with a separate current account for drawings only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that, in the absence of any instruction, capital accounts should be prepared by the fluctuating capital method.\nUnder this method there is a single capital account per partner, and all adjustments are posted into it.\nThe fixed capital method needs a specific agreement, because it requires two accounts per partner.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-064","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":64,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A firm whose deed provides interest on capital at 8% p.a. suffered a net loss of Rs. 10,000 during the year. No interest on capital will be credited to the partners.\nReason (R): Interest on capital is allowed only out of profits, so it is not allowed in a year in which the firm incurs a loss.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT (Anupam–Abhishek case) states that when the firm incurs a loss, no interest on capital is allowed, even though the deed provides for it.\nR is true. Interest on capital is an appropriation of profit, allowed only when the firm has earned profit.\nR states exactly why no interest is credited in A, so R correctly explains A.\nThe loss itself is shared in the profit sharing ratio.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-065","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":65,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Whose consent is needed to alter the clauses of a partnership deed?","image":"","options":["A majority of the partners","The partner with the largest capital","All the partners","The Registrar of Firms"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"As per NCERT, the clauses of a partnership deed can be altered only with the consent of all the partners.\nA majority decision is not enough, because the deed is based on the agreement of every partner.\nThe Registrar of Firms only registers the deed and has no power to change its terms.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-066","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":66,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Under the fixed capital method, in which account is a partner's drawings during the year recorded?","image":"","options":["Partner's Capital Account","Partner's Current Account","Profit and Loss Appropriation Account","Profit and Loss Adjustment Account"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Under the fixed capital method, two accounts are kept for each partner: a capital account and a current account.\nThe capital account records only additions to capital or permanent withdrawals of capital.\nDrawings, interest on drawings, salary, commission, interest on capital and share of profit or loss all go to the current account.\nThe capital account is the tempting option, but drawings there would make the capital fluctuate.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-067","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":67,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Meera, a partner, withdrew Rs. 20,000 at the beginning of each quarter during the year ending March 31, 2020. The partnership deed provides for interest on drawings at 10% p.a. What is the interest on her drawings?","image":"","options":["Rs. 4,000","Rs. 6,000","Rs. 5,000","Rs. 3,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: Total drawings = 4 × Rs. 20,000 = Rs. 80,000. The rate is 10% p.a. and each withdrawal is made at the beginning of the quarter.\nFormula: Average period = (12 + 3)/2 = 7.5 months.\nSubstituting: Interest = 80,000 × 10/100 × 7.5/12.\nSo interest on drawings = Rs. 5,000. Rs. 3,000 would be the result for end-of-quarter withdrawals (4.5 months).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-068","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":68,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Varun, a partner, withdrew Rs. 20,000 on July 1, 2023, Rs. 12,000 on November 1, 2023 and Rs. 18,000 on February 1, 2024. The books are closed on March 31, 2024. Interest on his drawings, calculated by the product method, amounted to Rs. 1,380. What rate of interest per annum was charged?","image":"","options":["5.52%","6%","7.2%","0.5%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Months to March 31: July 1 gives 9, Nov 1 gives 5, Feb 1 gives 2.\nProducts: $1{,}80{,}000 + 60{,}000 + 36{,}000 = 2{,}76{,}000$\nFormula: Interest = Products × Rate × $\\frac{1}{12}$, so Rate = $\\frac{1{,}380 \\times 12}{2{,}76{,}000} \\times 100$ = 6%.\n5.52% wrongly assumes 6 months on Rs. 50,000. 0.5% omits the $\\frac{1}{12}$ factor.\nSo rate = 6% p.a.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-069","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":69,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Tanvi's capital account showed a balance of Rs. 3,00,000 on April 1, 2019. On July 1, 2019 she withdrew Rs. 60,000 out of her capital. Interest on capital is allowed at 10% p.a. Books are closed on March 31. The interest on her capital for 2019-20 is:","image":"","options":["Rs. 30,000","Rs. 25,500","Rs. 24,000","Rs. 28,500"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: opening capital Rs. 3,00,000; withdrawal of Rs. 60,000 on July 1; rate 10% p.a.\nFormula: interest on opening capital till the withdrawal, then on the reduced capital for the rest of the year.\nSubstituting: $3,00,000 \\times \\frac{10}{100} \\times \\frac{3}{12} = 7,500$.\nReduced capital = Rs. 2,40,000, so $2,40,000 \\times \\frac{10}{100} \\times \\frac{9}{12} = 18,000$.\nSo interest = Rs. 25,500.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-070","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":70,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Asha, Bela and Chitra share profits in the ratio 3:2:1. Chitra is guaranteed a minimum profit of Rs. 30,000. Asha and Bela bear any deficiency in the ratio 3:2. The firm's net profit for the year is Rs. 1,50,000. What is Asha's final share of profit?","image":"","options":["Rs. 75,000","Rs. 72,000","Rs. 70,000","Rs. 73,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: profit Rs. 1,50,000, ratio 3:2:1, Chitra's guarantee Rs. 30,000.\nShares as per the ratio: Asha 3/6 × 1,50,000 = Rs. 75,000. Bela gets Rs. 50,000 and Chitra gets Rs. 25,000.\nDeficiency for Chitra = 30,000 − 25,000 = Rs. 5,000.\nAsha bears 3/5 × 5,000 = Rs. 3,000.\nSo Asha's final share = 75,000 − 3,000 = Rs. 72,000.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-071","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":71,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is a charge against profit and is therefore debited to the Profit and Loss Account, not to the Profit and Loss Appropriation Account?","image":"","options":["Interest on a loan advanced by a partner to the firm","Interest on partners' capitals","Salary payable to a working partner","Commission allowed to a partner"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A partner's loan is treated like a loan from an outsider. The Partnership Act allows 6% p.a. interest on it even when the deed is silent.\nThis interest must be paid whether the firm earns a profit or not, so it is a charge debited to the P&L A/c. In the Amitabh–Babul illustration, the interest on Amitabh's loan appears in the P&L A/c.\nInterest on capital, partner's salary and partner's commission are appropriations of profit. They are debited to the P&L Appropriation A/c.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-072","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":72,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Which section of the Indian Partnership Act 1932 defines partnership as the 'relation between persons who have agreed to share the profits of a business carried on by all or any of them acting for all'?","image":"","options":["Section 2","Section 4","Section 464","Section 6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Section 4 of the Indian Partnership Act 1932 gives this definition of partnership.\nSection 464 is a tempting choice, but it belongs to the Companies Act 2013, not the Partnership Act.\nThat section empowers the Central Government to prescribe the maximum number of partners in a firm.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-073","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":73,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Interest at 6% p.a. must be charged on a partner's drawings even if the partnership deed is silent on the matter.\nReason (R): Charging interest on drawings discourages partners from making excessive drawings.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A is false. Under the Indian Partnership Act 1932, no interest is charged on drawings unless the deed provides for it.\nThe 6% p.a. rule applies only to interest on a partner's loan to the firm.\nR is true. NCERT states that charging interest on drawings discourages excessive withdrawals by partners.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-074","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":74,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Profit and Loss Adjustment Account\n(B) Profit and Loss Appropriation Account\n(C) Partner's Current Account\n(D) Statement showing net effect\nList-II\n(i) Extension of the Profit and Loss Account showing how the year's profit is distributed among partners\n(ii) Shows, partner-wise, whether a capital account was credited in excess or short, before passing one direct entry\n(iii) Used to rectify omissions of earlier years without altering the old accounts\n(iv) Records drawings, interest and share of profit when capitals are fixed","image":"","options":["A-iii, B-i, C-iv, D-ii","A-i, B-iii, C-iv, D-ii","A-iii, B-i, C-ii, D-iv","A-ii, B-i, C-iv, D-iii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A)-(iii): The Profit and Loss Adjustment Account corrects past errors without reopening the old accounts.\n(B)-(i): The Appropriation Account is an extension of the Profit and Loss Account that distributes the year's profit.\n(C)-(iv): Under the fixed capital method, current items such as drawings, interest and share of profit go to the Current Account.\n(D)-(ii): The net-effect statement compares what should have been credited or debited with what actually was, showing excess or short.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-075","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":75,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Sahil, a partner, made two kinds of withdrawals during the year ended March 31, 2024:\n(i) Rs. 6,000 at the beginning of each quarter for business-unrelated household needs, and\n(ii) Rs. 2,000 at the end of each month for personal expenses.\nThe deed provides interest on drawings at 10% p.a. What is the total interest on Sahil's drawings for the year?","image":"","options":["Rs. 2,400","Rs. 2,600","Rs. 2,800","Rs. 2,200"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Quarterly drawings: 6,000 × 4 = Rs. 24,000 for 7.5 months, giving 24,000 × 10/100 × 7.5/12 = Rs. 1,500.\nMonthly drawings: 2,000 × 12 = Rs. 24,000 for 5.5 months, giving 24,000 × 10/100 × 5.5/12 = Rs. 1,100.\nTotal interest = 1,500 + 1,100 = Rs. 2,600.\nRs. 2,400 would come from treating all Rs. 48,000 as outstanding for an average of 6 months.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-076","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":76,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Asha and Bhuvan share profits in the ratio 3:2, with fixed capitals of Rs. 2,00,000 and Rs. 1,00,000. After the year's profit had been distributed, three errors were found:\n(i) interest on capital @ 6% p.a. was omitted;\n(ii) interest on drawings, Asha Rs. 1,500 and Bhuvan Rs. 500, was not charged;\n(iii) Bhuvan's salary of Rs. 10,000 was not provided.\nWhat amount will be debited to Asha's capital account in the single adjusting entry?","image":"","options":["Rs. 5,100","Rs. 4,800","Rs. 900","Rs. 15,600"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: interest on capital is Asha 12,000 and Bhuvan 6,000.\nNet reduction in profit = 18,000 + 10,000 − 2,000 = Rs. 26,000. Shared 3:2, this is Asha 15,600 and Bhuvan 10,400 (debit).\nAsha's net effect = 12,000 Cr − 1,500 Dr − 15,600 Dr = Dr 5,100.\nBhuvan's net effect = 16,000 Cr − 500 Dr − 10,400 Dr = Cr 5,100.\nThe entry is: Asha's Capital Dr 5,100, To Bhuvan's Capital 5,100. Ignoring interest on drawings would wrongly give Rs. 4,800.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-077","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":77,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"The net profit of a firm before charging partner Kiran's commission is Rs 2,20,000. The deed allows Kiran a commission of 10% of the net profit after charging such commission. What is the amount of Kiran's commission?","image":"","options":["Rs 22,000","Rs 24,200","Rs 18,000","Rs 20,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: Profit before commission = Rs 2,20,000. Commission = 10% of the profit after commission.\nFormula: Let profit after commission = P. Then P + 0.10P = 2,20,000. So commission = Profit before commission × $\\frac{10}{110}$.\nSubstituting: 2,20,000 × $\\frac{10}{110}$ = 20,000.\nCheck: Profit after commission = 2,00,000, and 10% of 2,00,000 = 20,000.\nRs 22,000 is wrong because it takes 10% of the profit before commission.\nSo the commission = Rs 20,000.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-078","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":78,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Kiran, a partner, withdrew Rs. 20,000 at the beginning of each quarter during the year ending March 31, 2020. The deed provides for interest on drawings at 9% p.a. Calculate the interest on Kiran's drawings.","image":"","options":["Rs. 4,500","Rs. 2,700","Rs. 3,600","Rs. 7,200"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: 4 drawings × Rs. 20,000 = Rs. 80,000, made at the beginning of each quarter, at 9% p.a.\nFormula: average period = (12 + 9 + 6 + 3)/4 = 7.5 months.\nSubstituting: 80,000 × 9/100 × 7.5/12 = Rs. 4,500.\nRs. 2,700 uses 4.5 months, which applies only to drawings at the end of each quarter.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-079","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":79,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Xavier and Yogesh share profits in the ratio 3:2. Their capitals are Rs. 3,00,000 and Rs. 1,00,000. The deed allows interest on capital at 10% p.a. The firm's profit for the year, before interest on capital, is only Rs. 20,000. What amount will be credited to Xavier as interest on capital?","image":"","options":["Rs. 15,000","Rs. 10,000","Rs. 12,000","Rs. 30,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Interest due: Xavier = Rs. 30,000 and Yogesh = Rs. 10,000, a total of Rs. 40,000.\nProfit (Rs. 20,000) is less than the interest due, so interest is restricted to the available profit.\nThe profit is shared in the ratio of interest on capital, 3:1, not in the profit sharing ratio.\nXavier = $20,000 \\times \\frac{3}{4}$ = Rs. 15,000.\nRs. 12,000 wrongly uses the 3:2 profit sharing ratio.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-080","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":80,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Xavier, Yamini and Zoya share profits in the ratio 5:3:2. Their capitals are Rs 80,000, Rs 60,000 and Rs 40,000, and the deed allows 6% p.a. interest on capital.\nXavier gets a salary of Rs 1,500 per month, and Yamini gets a commission of Rs 6,000. Interest on drawings is Xavier Rs 600, Yamini Rs 400 and Zoya Rs 200. The net profit as per the P&L A/c is Rs 73,600.\nWhat is the total amount credited to Yamini's capital account from the Profit and Loss Appropriation Account?","image":"","options":["Rs 21,200","Rs 15,600","Rs 22,000","Rs 21,600"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: Net profit Rs 73,600; total interest on drawings Rs 1,200.\nStep 1 (debits): Salary 18,000 + commission 6,000 + interest on capital (4,800 + 3,600 + 2,400 = 10,800) = 34,800.\nStep 2: Divisible profit = 73,600 + 1,200 − 34,800 = 40,000. Yamini's share = 40,000 × $\\frac{3}{10}$ = 12,000.\nStep 3: Credits to Yamini = commission 6,000 + interest on capital 3,600 + share 12,000 = 21,600.\nRs 21,200 wrongly nets off her interest on drawings of 400, which is a separate debit to her capital account.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-081","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":81,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Kiran's capital account showed Rs. 4,00,000 on April 1, 2019. She introduced additional capital of Rs. 1,00,000 on July 1, 2019 and withdrew Rs. 60,000 of capital on January 1, 2020. The deed allows interest on capital at 9% p.a., and the firm earned an adequate profit. Books are closed on March 31 every year. What is the interest on Kiran's capital for 2019-20?","image":"","options":["Rs. 42,750","Rs. 36,000","Rs. 39,600","Rs. 41,400"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: Capital is Rs. 4,00,000 for 3 months, Rs. 5,00,000 for 6 months and Rs. 4,40,000 for 3 months, at 9% p.a.\nFormula: Interest = Capital × Rate × Period/12 for each block.\nSubstituting: 4,00,000 × 9% × 3/12 = 9,000; 5,00,000 × 9% × 6/12 = 22,500; 4,40,000 × 9% × 3/12 = 9,900.\nSo total interest = Rs. 41,400. Rs. 42,750 ignores the withdrawal.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-082","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":82,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"A partnership firm has no written or oral agreement on the matters below. How many of the following positions are correct as per the Indian Partnership Act 1932?\n(i) A partner who contributed Rs. 5,00,000 can be credited with interest on capital at 6% p.a.\n(ii) A partner who contributed Rs. 1,00,000 shares profits equally with a partner who contributed Rs. 4,00,000.\n(iii) Interest at 6% p.a. can be charged on the drawings of each partner.\n(iv) A partner who secretly runs a shop competing with the firm's business may keep the profit of that shop.\n(v) A partner who rented out the firm's delivery van privately must pay the rent earned to the firm.\n(vi) A partner's loan to the firm carries interest at 6% p.a.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Incorrect: no interest on capital is allowed unless expressly agreed.\n(ii) Correct: profits are shared equally, irrespective of capital.\n(iii) Incorrect: no interest is charged on drawings if the deed is silent.\n(iv) Incorrect: profit from a competing business must be paid to the firm.\n(v) Correct: personal profit from using firm property must be handed over to the firm.\n(vi) Correct: a partner's loan carries interest at 6% p.a.\nCorrect positions: (ii), (v) and (vi) = 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-083","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":83,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Nikhil, a partner in a trading firm, had a capital balance of Rs. 4,00,000 on April 1, 2019. On July 1, 2019 he introduced further capital of Rs. 80,000. On January 1, 2020 he withdrew Rs. 1,20,000 from his capital. The deed allows interest on capital at 6% p.a. The firm earned a sufficient profit for the year ended March 31, 2020. Interest on Nikhil's capital is:","image":"","options":["Rs. 24,000","Rs. 21,600","Rs. 27,000","Rs. 25,800"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Split the year by the capital balance in each period:\nApril–June: $4,00,000 \\times \\frac{6}{100} \\times \\frac{3}{12} = 6,000$.\nJuly–December: capital Rs. 4,80,000, so $4,80,000 \\times \\frac{6}{100} \\times \\frac{6}{12} = 14,400$.\nJanuary–March: capital Rs. 3,60,000, so $3,60,000 \\times \\frac{6}{100} \\times \\frac{3}{12} = 5,400$.\nSo total interest = Rs. 25,800.\nProfit is sufficient, so the full interest is allowed.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-084","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":84,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (timing of a fixed withdrawal) with List-II (average period used for interest on total drawings):\nList-I\n(A) Beginning of each month\n(B) End of each month\n(C) Beginning of each quarter\n(D) End of each quarter\nList-II\n(i) 4½ months\n(ii) 5½ months\n(iii) 6½ months\n(iv) 7½ months","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Beginning of month: $\\frac{12+1}{2} = 6\\frac{1}{2}$ months → (iii).\n(B) End of month: $\\frac{11+0}{2} = 5\\frac{1}{2}$ months → (ii).\n(C) Beginning of quarter: $\\frac{12+3}{2} = 7\\frac{1}{2}$ months → (iv).\n(D) End of quarter: $\\frac{9+0}{2} = 4\\frac{1}{2}$ months → (i).\nOption (4) swaps the two quarterly cases.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-085","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":85,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Kiran's capital on April 1, 2023 was Rs. 4,00,000. She introduced additional capital of Rs. 1,00,000 on October 1, 2023. The deed allows interest on capital at 6% p.a. Books are closed on March 31 every year. What is the interest on Kiran's capital for 2023-24?","image":"","options":["Rs. 24,000","Rs. 27,000","Rs. 30,000","Rs. 25,500"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: opening capital Rs. 4,00,000 for 12 months; additional capital Rs. 1,00,000 from October 1 to March 31, which is 6 months; rate 6% p.a.\nFormula: Interest = Capital × Rate × Time.\nSubstituting:\n- On opening capital: 4,00,000 × 6/100 = Rs. 24,000\n- On additional capital: 1,00,000 × 6/100 × 6/12 = Rs. 3,000\nSo total interest = Rs. 27,000.\nOption (3) wrongly charges interest on the additional capital for the full year.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-086","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":86,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Kiran and Tara are partners. Their partnership deed is silent on all the matters below. Kiran contributed Rs. 3,00,000 as capital and Tara contributed Rs. 1,00,000.\n(i) Kiran, who manages daily operations, claims a salary of Rs. 8,000 per month.\n(ii) Tara, who lent Rs. 1,00,000 to the firm, claims interest on it at 6% p.a.\n(iii) Tara claims an equal share in profits.\n(iv) Kiran claims interest on capital at 5% p.a.\n(v) Kiran wants interest at 6% p.a. charged on Tara's drawings.\nHow many of the above claims are valid?","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Invalid: no salary is payable to a partner unless the deed provides for it.\n(ii) Valid: interest on a partner's loan is allowed at 6% p.a. when the deed is silent.\n(iii) Valid: profits are shared equally, irrespective of the capital contributed.\n(iv) Invalid: no interest on capital is allowed without an express agreement.\n(v) Invalid: no interest is charged on drawings without an express agreement.\nSo 2 claims are valid.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-087","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":87,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Xavier and Yash are partners. The deed provides for interest on drawings at 9% p.a. During the year ended March 31, 2024:\n- Xavier withdrew Rs. 8,000 at the beginning of every month.\n- Yash withdrew Rs. 24,000 at the end of every quarter.\nBoth partners withdrew the same total amount during the year. By how much does the interest charged on Xavier's drawings exceed the interest charged on Yash's drawings?","image":"","options":["Rs. 1,440","Nil, as total drawings are equal","Rs. 720","Rs. 2,880"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Xavier withdrew 8,000 × 12 = Rs. 96,000; Yash withdrew 24,000 × 4 = Rs. 96,000.\nXavier (beginning of month): average period = 6½ months.\nInterest = $96{,}000 \\times \\frac{9}{100} \\times \\frac{6.5}{12}$ = Rs. 4,680.\nYash (end of quarter): average period = 4½ months.\nInterest = $96{,}000 \\times \\frac{9}{100} \\times \\frac{4.5}{12}$ = Rs. 3,240.\nDifference = 4,680 − 3,240 = Rs. 1,440. Equal totals do not mean equal interest, because the periods differ.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-088","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":88,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (items, fixed capital method) with List-II (where they are shown):\nList-I\n(a) Interest on drawings\n(b) Fresh capital introduced by a partner\n(c) Debit balance of a partner's current account\n(d) Balance of a partner's capital account\nList-II\n(i) Liabilities side of Balance Sheet\n(ii) Debit side of partner's current account\n(iii) Credit side of partner's capital account\n(iv) Assets side of Balance Sheet","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Interest on drawings is a charge on the partner, so it is debited to the current account: (ii).\n(b) Fresh capital introduced is credited to the capital account: (iii).\n(c) A debit balance in the current account is shown on the assets side: (iv).\n(d) The fixed capital always has a credit balance, shown on the liabilities side: (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-089","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":89,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about a partnership agreement is correct?","image":"","options":["It must be in writing to be valid","An oral agreement is equally valid, though a written agreement is preferred to avoid disputes","It is valid only if registered with the Registrar of Firms","It is valid only if at least one partner contributes capital"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Partnership arises from an agreement, which may be oral or written.\nThe Partnership Act does not require the agreement to be in writing, so an oral agreement is equally valid.\nA written agreement (the deed) is preferred to avoid disputes.\nRegistration of the deed is only 'preferable', not a condition of validity.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-090","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":90,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Arjun and Bela are equal partners with capitals of Rs. 80,000 and Rs. 40,000 respectively. After the year's profit was distributed, it was found that interest on capital @ 5% p.a., provided in the deed, had been omitted. By what amount should Arjun's capital account be credited in the single adjusting entry?","image":"","options":["Rs. 1,000","Rs. 2,000","Rs. 3,000","Rs. 4,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: interest due is Arjun 5% of 80,000 = Rs. 4,000 and Bela 5% of 40,000 = Rs. 2,000, a total of Rs. 6,000.\nHad interest been allowed, profit would have been Rs. 6,000 less. That reduction is shared equally, so each partner loses Rs. 3,000.\nArjun's net effect = 4,000 (Cr) − 3,000 (Dr) = Rs. 1,000 short.\nSo Arjun is credited Rs. 1,000 and Bela is debited Rs. 1,000.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-091","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":91,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A partner can bind the other partners by acts done by him in the course of the firm's business.\nReason (R): Each partner carrying on the business is the principal as well as the agent for all the other partners.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Because of mutual agency, a partner's acts relating to the firm's business bind all the other partners.\nR is true. Each partner acts as an agent who binds the others and as a principal who is bound by their acts.\nR is exactly why A holds, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-092","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":92,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"On March 31, 2020, after closing the books, Ravi's capital account showed a balance of Rs. 2,10,000. During 2019-20:\n- his drawings of Rs. 30,000 had been debited to his capital account;\n- his share of profit of Rs. 40,000 had been credited;\n- he had introduced additional capital of Rs. 20,000 on October 1, 2019.\nInterest on capital is to be allowed at 10% p.a. What is the interest on Ravi's capital for the year?","image":"","options":["Rs. 19,000","Rs. 21,000","Rs. 18,000","Rs. 20,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Opening capital = Closing capital + Drawings − Share of profit − Additional capital.\nOpening capital = 2,10,000 + 30,000 − 40,000 − 20,000 = 1,80,000.\nInterest on opening capital for the full year = 1,80,000 × 10% = 18,000.\nInterest on the additional capital for 6 months = 20,000 × 10/100 × 6/12 = 1,000.\nSo total interest = Rs. 19,000.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-093","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":93,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Look at the proforma of a Partner's Capital Account prepared under the fixed capital method. Which of the following items appears on its DEBIT side (other than the closing balance)?","image":"","options":["Drawings","Share of loss from Profit and Loss Appropriation","Bank (permanent withdrawal of capital)","Interest on drawings"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Under the fixed capital method, the capital account contains only four things:\n- Opening balance (Cr.) and fresh capital introduced (Cr.).\n- Permanent withdrawal of capital (Dr.) and closing balance (Dr.).\nDrawings, interest on drawings and share of loss are debited to the partner's current account, not the capital account.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-094","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":94,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: When an omission is rectified by a single adjusting entry directly in partners' capital accounts, the total of all partners' capitals remains unchanged.\nStatement II: In the case of equal partners Rameez (capital Rs. 50,000) and Zaheer (capital Rs. 1,00,000), where interest on capital @ 6% was omitted, Rameez's capital account is debited because the interest due to him (Rs. 3,000) is less than his share (Rs. 4,500) of the reduction in profit.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The entry debits some partners and credits others by the same total. It only transfers amounts between partners, so total capital does not change.\nStatement II is correct. Total interest = 3,000 + 6,000 = Rs. 9,000, and each partner bears Rs. 4,500 of it.\nRameez's net effect = 3,000 − 4,500 = Rs. 1,500 excess, so he is debited.\nThe entry is: Rameez's Capital Dr. 1,500, To Zaheer's Capital 1,500.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-095","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":95,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Under the fixed capital method, the partners' capital accounts always show a credit balance.\nReason (R): Under the fixed capital method, drawings, interest on drawings and share of loss are recorded in the partners' current accounts and not in their capital accounts.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. The fixed capital account carries only capital introduced and permanent withdrawals, so it always shows a credit balance.\nR is true. The debit items (drawings, interest on drawings, losses) are posted to the current account.\nR explains A: these debit items are kept out of the capital account, so the capital account never turns debit. The current account is the one that may show a debit balance.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-096","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":96,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (timing of fixed drawings over one year) with List-II (average period for interest on drawings):\nList-I\n(a) At the beginning of each month\n(b) In the middle of each month\n(c) At the end of each month\n(d) At the beginning of each quarter\nList-II\n(i) 6 months\n(ii) 7.5 months\n(iii) 6.5 months\n(iv) 5.5 months","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Beginning of each month: (12 + 1)/2 = 6.5 months, so (iii).\n(b) Middle of each month: (11.5 + 0.5)/2 = 6 months, so (i).\n(c) End of each month: (11 + 0)/2 = 5.5 months, so (iv).\n(d) Beginning of each quarter: (12 + 3)/2 = 7.5 months, so (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-097","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":97,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Pran and Qadir share profits in the ratio 3 : 1. They admit Ravi for a $\\frac{1}{5}$ share. Pran and Qadir will share the remaining profit in their old ratio. Ravi is guaranteed a minimum profit of Rs. 40,000, and any deficiency is to be borne by Pran and Qadir in their old ratio. The net profit for the year is Rs. 1,50,000. What amount of profit will finally be credited to Pran?","image":"","options":["Rs. 90,000","Rs. 80,000","Rs. 85,000","Rs. 82,500"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: profit Rs. 1,50,000, Ravi's share $\\frac{1}{5}$, guarantee Rs. 40,000.\nNew shares: Pran = $\\frac{4}{5}\\times\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{3}{5}$, Qadir = $\\frac{1}{5}$, Ravi = $\\frac{1}{5}$.\nPran's share = 90,000. Qadir's share = 30,000. Ravi's share = 30,000.\nDeficiency = 40,000 − 30,000 = 10,000, borne 3:1. Pran bears 7,500 and Qadir bears 2,500.\nSo Pran gets 90,000 − 7,500 = Rs. 82,500.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-098","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":98,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): If the firm's profit is less than the total interest on capital due to the partners, the profit is effectively distributed in the ratio of interest on capital.\nReason (R): Interest on capital is a charge against profit and must be allowed in full even if the firm suffers a loss.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. Interest is restricted to the available profit, so partners receive the profit in the ratio of their interest on capital. For example, interest of 12,000 and 16,000 gives a 3:4 ratio.\nR is false. Interest on capital is an appropriation of profit, not a charge against it. No interest on capital is allowed when the firm incurs a loss.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-099","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":99,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): According to NCERT, if a firm suffers a net loss for the year, interest on capital and salary are not allowed to the partners, and the whole loss is shared in the profit sharing ratio.\nReason (R): Interest on capital and partner's salary are appropriations of profit, not charges against profit.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT notes that when the firm suffers a loss, no interest on capital, salary or remuneration is allowed. In the Yadu–Madhu–Vidu illustration, the net loss of Rs 75,000 is simply divided in the ratio 2:2:1.\nR is true. These items are distributions out of profit, so they are made through the P&L Appropriation A/c.\nR explains A: when there is no profit, there is nothing to appropriate.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-100","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":100,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When only the total amount of drawings for the year is given and the dates are not specified, interest on drawings is calculated for 6 months.\nReason (R): In such a case, it is assumed that the amount was withdrawn evenly throughout the year.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT takes 6 months as the average period when withdrawal dates are not known. An example is Shakila's Rs. 60,000 at 8%, which gives Rs. 2,400.\nR is true: the drawings are assumed to be spread evenly across the year, roughly in the middle of each month.\nThis even-spread assumption is exactly why the average period works out to 6 months, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-101","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":101,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Farhan's capital on April 1, 2019 was Rs. 4,00,000. He introduced additional capital of Rs. 1,20,000 on October 1, 2019. The deed allows interest on capital at 6% p.a. Books are closed on March 31 every year. Interest on Farhan's capital for 2019-20 is:","image":"","options":["Rs. 24,000","Rs. 31,200","Rs. 27,600","Rs. 25,800"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: opening capital Rs. 4,00,000; addition Rs. 1,20,000 on October 1; rate 6% p.a.\nFormula: interest on opening capital for 12 months, plus interest on the addition from October 1 to March 31 (6 months).\nSubstituting: $4,00,000 \\times \\frac{6}{100} = 24,000$ and $1,20,000 \\times \\frac{6}{100} \\times \\frac{6}{12} = 3,600$.\nSo total interest = Rs. 27,600.\nRs. 31,200 wrongly charges interest on the addition for a full year.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-102","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":102,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"In the books of a partnership firm, the Profit and Loss Appropriation Account is best described as:","image":"","options":["A substitute for the Profit and Loss Account prepared only when the firm earns a profit","An extension of the Profit and Loss Account that shows how the net profit is distributed among the partners","A personal account of each partner that replaces the capital account","An account that records only the expenses paid to outsiders, such as the manager's commission"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT describes the Profit and Loss Appropriation Account as merely an extension of the Profit and Loss Account.\nIt begins with the net profit or net loss as per the P&L Account. Interest on capital, interest on drawings, salary and commission to partners are adjusted through it.\nIt does not replace the P&L Account, because the net profit must be found first.\nExpenses paid to outsiders, such as the manager's commission, belong in the P&L Account, not in the appropriation account.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-103","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":103,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Asha and Bina are equal partners with fixed capitals of Rs. 2,40,000 and Rs. 1,60,000 respectively. After the final accounts were prepared, it was found that interest on capital at 5% p.a., as provided in the deed, had not been credited before the profits were distributed. Which single adjusting entry correctly rectifies this error?","image":"","options":["Asha's Capital A/c Dr. 2,000 To Bina's Capital A/c 2,000","Bina's Capital A/c Dr. 2,000 To Asha's Capital A/c 2,000","Bina's Capital A/c Dr. 4,000 To Asha's Capital A/c 4,000","Profit and Loss Adjustment A/c Dr. 20,000 To Asha's and Bina's Capital A/cs 20,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Interest that should have been credited: Asha 5% of 2,40,000 = Rs. 12,000 (Cr.); Bina 5% of 1,60,000 = Rs. 8,000 (Cr.).\nThe Rs. 20,000 was instead distributed equally as profit, so it must be reversed at Rs. 10,000 (Dr.) each.\nNet effect: Asha 12,000 − 10,000 = Cr. 2,000 (short). Bina 8,000 − 10,000 = Dr. 2,000 (excess).\nSo debit Bina and credit Asha with Rs. 2,000. Option (4) is only the first step of the P&L Adjustment method, not a single adjusting entry.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-104","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":104,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following items are debited to the Profit and Loss Appropriation Account of a firm?\n(i) Interest on partners' capital\n(ii) Interest on a partner's loan to the firm\n(iii) Salary to a partner\n(iv) Commission to the manager\n(v) Interest on partners' drawings\n(vi) Commission to a partner","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Debited: interest on capital is an appropriation. \n(ii) Not debited: interest on a partner's loan is a charge, debited to the P&L A/c. \n(iii) Debited: partner's salary is an appropriation. \n(iv) Not debited: manager's commission is an expense, charged to the P&L A/c. \n(v) Not debited: interest on drawings is credited to the appropriation account. \n(vi) Debited: partner's commission is an appropriation. \nSo three items, (i), (iii) and (vi), are debited. \nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-105","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":105,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Arjun's capitals are fluctuating. His capital on April 1, 2019 was Rs. 3,00,000. On October 1, 2019 he introduced additional capital of Rs. 1,00,000. Interest on capital is allowed @ 6% p.a., and the firm earned a profit. Other details for 2019-20:\n- Salary: Rs. 24,000\n- Share of profit: Rs. 30,000\n- Drawings: Rs. 40,000\n- Interest on drawings: Rs. 1,200\nWhat is the balance of Arjun's Capital Account on March 31, 2020?","image":"","options":["Rs. 4,33,800","Rs. 4,36,800","Rs. 4,36,200","Rs. 4,73,800"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Interest on capital = 3,00,000 × 6% + 1,00,000 × 6% × 6/12 = 18,000 + 3,000 = Rs. 21,000.\nUnder the fluctuating method, all items go to the capital account.\nCredits = 3,00,000 + 1,00,000 + 21,000 + 24,000 + 30,000 = 4,75,000.\nDebits = 40,000 + 1,200 = 41,200.\nSo closing capital = Rs. 4,33,800 (Cr.).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-106","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":106,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Leena, Manav and Nisha share profits in the ratio 2 : 2 : 1. Leena alone guarantees Nisha a minimum profit of Rs. 50,000 a year. The firm's profit for the year is Rs. 2,00,000. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Leena's final share of profit is Rs. 70,000.\n(ii) Manav's share remains Rs. 80,000.\n(iii) Nisha is credited with Rs. 50,000.\n(iv) Manav bears Rs. 5,000 of the deficiency.\n(v) If the profit had been Rs. 3,00,000, Leena would still have to bear a deficiency.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Shares at 2:2:1: Leena 80,000, Manav 80,000, Nisha 40,000. Deficiency = 10,000.\n(i) Correct: Leena gets 80,000 − 10,000 = 70,000.\n(ii) Correct: Manav gave no guarantee, so his share is unaffected.\n(iii) Correct: Nisha gets 40,000 + 10,000 = 50,000.\n(iv) Incorrect: Leena bears the whole deficiency.\n(v) Incorrect: Nisha's share would be 60,000, which is more than 50,000, so no deficiency arises.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-107","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":107,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Under the fixed capital method, a partner's capital account always shows a credit balance.\nReason (R): Under this method, drawings, interest on drawings, salary, interest on capital and share of profit or loss are recorded in the partner's current account, not in the capital account.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: under the fixed capital method, the capital account changes only when capital is added or permanently withdrawn, so it always shows a credit balance.\nR is true: all other partner items, including drawings and losses, go to a separate current account.\nR explains A. Because drawings and losses never hit the capital account, nothing can turn its balance into a debit. Any debit balance appears only in the current account.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-108","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":108,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Xavier and Yash share profits in the ratio 3 : 2. They admit Zoya for a $\\frac{1}{5}$ share in profits. Xavier and Yash continue to share the remaining profit between themselves in their old ratio. What is Xavier's share in the new profit sharing ratio?","image":"","options":["$\\frac{3}{5}$","$\\frac{9}{25}$","$\\frac{12}{25}$","$\\frac{2}{5}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: Zoya's share is $\\frac{1}{5}$, and the old ratio is 3:2.\nRemaining profit = $1-\\frac{1}{5}=\\frac{4}{5}$.\nXavier's share = $\\frac{4}{5}\\times\\frac{3}{5}=\\frac{12}{25}$. Yash's share = $\\frac{8}{25}$. Zoya's share = $\\frac{5}{25}$.\nSo the new ratio is 12 : 8 : 5.\n$\\frac{3}{5}$ ignores Zoya's admission.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-109","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":109,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Meera and Kunal jointly purchased a plot of land in 2022 as a long-term investment and did nothing else with it. In 2024 they started regularly buying plots and selling them for profit, sharing the gains equally.\nWhich of the following correctly describes their relationship?","image":"","options":["Partners in both 2022 and 2024","Co-owners in 2022 and partners in 2024","Partners in 2022 and co-owners in 2024","Co-owners in both years, since no written deed exists"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In 2022 they merely owned the plot jointly, with no business. Mere co-ownership of property does not amount to partnership, so they were co-owners.\nIn 2024 they carried on the business of buying and selling land for profit and shared the gains. This makes them partners.\nA written deed is not required, so option (4) fails.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-110","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":110,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Sonal and Ritu are partners with no partnership deed. On April 1, 2019, Sonal advanced a loan of Rs. 50,000 to the firm and did not withdraw it during the year. Sonal claims interest at 10% p.a. How much interest on the loan is Sonal entitled to for the year ending March 31, 2020?","image":"","options":["Rs. 3,000","Rs. 5,000","Rs. 6,000","Nil, as the deed is silent"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: loan = Rs. 50,000 for 12 months, and there is no deed.\nRule: if the deed is silent, the Indian Partnership Act 1932 allows interest on a partner's loan at 6% p.a. Sonal's claim of 10% is not valid.\nSubstituting: 50,000 × 6/100 = Rs. 3,000.\nThe 'Nil' option is wrong because interest on capital is not allowed without a deed, but interest on a loan is allowed at 6%.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-111","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":111,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Persons who have entered into partnership with one another are collectively called:","image":"","options":["Partners","Firm's name","Firm","Company"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"As per NCERT, the persons in a partnership are individually called 'partners' and collectively called the 'firm'.\nThe name under which the business is carried on is the 'firm's name'. It is not the name for the group of persons.\nA company is a different form of organisation.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-112","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":112,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Ravi advanced a loan of Rs. 1,00,000 to his firm. The partnership deed is silent on interest on loan, so Ravi is entitled to interest of Rs. 6,000 for the full year.\nReason (R): Under the Indian Partnership Act 1932, a partner who advances a loan to the firm for business is entitled to interest at 6% per annum when there is no agreement.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true. When the deed is silent, the Partnership Act allows interest on a partner's loan at 6% p.a.\nA is true. Interest = 6% of Rs. 1,00,000 = Rs. 6,000 for the year.\nA follows directly from the provision stated in R, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-113","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":113,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Xavier and Yamini share profits and losses in the ratio 3 : 1. Their deed provides:\n- a salary of Rs. 12,000 p.a. to Xavier\n- interest on capital at 5% p.a.\n- interest on drawings\n\nFor the year, interest on drawings is Xavier Rs. 500 and Yamini Rs. 300. The Profit and Loss Account shows a net loss of Rs. 30,000. What share of loss will be debited to Xavier's capital account through the Profit and Loss Appropriation Account?","image":"","options":["Rs. 22,500","Rs. 30,900","Rs. 23,100","Rs. 21,900"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Since the firm has a net loss, no salary and no interest on capital are allowed. \nInterest on drawings is still charged to the partners and credited to the appropriation account. This reduces the loss: 30,000 − (500 + 300) = Rs. 29,200. \nXavier's share = $\\frac{3}{4}$ × 29,200 = Rs. 21,900. \nRs. 30,900 wrongly includes the salary, giving a loss of 41,200. Rs. 23,100 wrongly adds the interest on drawings to the loss. \nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-114","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":114,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Aman and Bala formed a partnership on April 1, 2024 without any partnership deed. They contributed capitals of Rs. 3,00,000 and Rs. 1,00,000 respectively. On October 1, 2024, Aman also advanced a loan of Rs. 2,00,000 to the firm. The firm's profit for the year ended March 31, 2025, before any interest on the loan, was Rs. 1,00,000. Aman claims interest on his capital at 10% p.a. and wants profits shared in the capital ratio. What is Bala's correct share of profit?","image":"","options":["Rs. 47,000","Rs. 23,500","Rs. 44,000","Rs. 50,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: there is no deed, so the Indian Partnership Act, 1932 applies.\nNo interest on capital is allowed, and profits are shared equally. Aman's claim for capital-ratio sharing is therefore invalid.\nInterest on loan at 6% p.a. for 6 months = 2,00,000 × 6/100 × 6/12 = Rs. 6,000. This is a charge against profit.\nProfit left for distribution = 1,00,000 − 6,000 = Rs. 94,000.\nShared equally, Bala's share = 94,000 ÷ 2 = Rs. 47,000.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-115","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":115,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Ritu and Sahil are partners with no partnership deed. Ritu's loan of Rs. 50,000 to the firm was outstanding for the whole year. The firm's Profit and Loss Account shows a loss of Rs. 20,000 before considering any interest on Ritu's loan. What is each partner's share of the final loss?","image":"","options":["Rs. 10,000","Rs. 8,500","Rs. 11,500","Rs. 23,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: the deed is silent, so the loan earns interest at 6% p.a. and losses are shared equally.\nInterest on loan = 50,000 × 6/100 = Rs. 3,000. It is a charge against profit, not an appropriation, so it is allowed even in a loss year.\nFinal loss = 20,000 + 3,000 = Rs. 23,000.\nEach partner's share = 23,000 ÷ 2 = Rs. 11,500.\nRs. 10,000 wrongly ignores the interest. Rs. 23,000 is the total loss, not each partner's share.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-116","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":116,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"X, Y and Z are partners sharing profits in the ratio 5:3:2. Their capitals are Rs. 4,00,000, Rs. 3,00,000 and Rs. 1,00,000 respectively, and interest on capital is allowed at 5% p.a. Z is guaranteed a minimum share of profit of Rs. 60,000 (excluding interest on capital). Any deficiency is to be borne by X and Y in the ratio 3:1. The net profit before interest on capital is Rs. 2,80,000. What is X's final share of profit (excluding interest on capital)?","image":"","options":["Rs. 1,11,000","Rs. 1,12,500","Rs. 1,14,000","Rs. 1,08,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1: interest on capital = 20,000 + 15,000 + 5,000 = Rs. 40,000. Divisible profit = 2,80,000 − 40,000 = Rs. 2,40,000.\nStep 2: shares in 5:3:2 are X Rs. 1,20,000, Y Rs. 72,000 and Z Rs. 48,000.\nStep 3: Z's deficiency = 60,000 − 48,000 = Rs. 12,000. X bears 3/4 of it, which is Rs. 9,000.\nStep 4: X's final share = 1,20,000 − 9,000 = Rs. 1,11,000.\nRs. 1,12,500 wrongly splits the deficiency in the ratio 5:3 instead of the agreed ratio 3:1.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-117","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":117,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"The books of a firm are closed on December 31 every year. During 2023, partner Arjun withdrew Rs. 6,000 on February 1, Rs. 4,000 on June 30 and Rs. 9,000 on September 30. Interest on drawings is charged at 12% p.a. What is the interest on Arjun's drawings?","image":"","options":["Rs. 1,020","Rs. 1,140","Rs. 1,170","Rs. 1,710"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: Year-end = December 31, 2023; rate = 12% p.a.\nMonths to December 31: Feb 1 gives 11, June 30 gives 6, Sept 30 gives 3.\nProducts: $66{,}000 + 24{,}000 + 27{,}000 = 1{,}17{,}000$\nInterest = $1{,}17{,}000 \\times \\frac{12}{100} \\times \\frac{1}{12} = 1{,}170$\nRs. 1,020 is the trap: it counts months to March 31 instead of the actual closing date.\nSo interest = Rs. 1,170.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-118","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":118,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Amar, Bhanu and Chetan are partners in a consultancy firm sharing profits in the ratio 5:3:2. The deed provides the following:\n(i) Interest on capital at 5% p.a. on capitals of Rs. 10,00,000 (Amar), Rs. 4,00,000 (Bhanu) and Rs. 2,00,000 (Chetan).\n(ii) Amar guarantees that he will earn fees of at least Rs. 4,00,000 for the firm. He actually earned Rs. 2,50,000.\n(iii) Chetan is guaranteed a profit of Rs. 1,60,000, excluding interest on capital. Any deficiency is borne by Amar and Bhanu equally.\nThe net profit as per the Profit and Loss Account is Rs. 6,00,000. What is Bhanu's final share of profit (excluding interest on capital)?","image":"","options":["Rs. 1,88,000","Rs. 2,01,000","Rs. 1,91,250","Rs. 1,28,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Fee shortfall made good by Amar = 4,00,000 − 2,50,000 = Rs. 1,50,000. This is credited to the Appropriation Account.\nInterest on capital = 50,000 + 20,000 + 10,000 = Rs. 80,000.\nDivisible profit = 6,00,000 + 1,50,000 − 80,000 = Rs. 6,70,000.\nChetan's share = 2/10 × 6,70,000 = Rs. 1,34,000, so the deficiency = 1,60,000 − 1,34,000 = Rs. 26,000. Each guarantor bears Rs. 13,000.\nBhanu = 3/10 × 6,70,000 − 13,000 = 2,01,000 − 13,000 = Rs. 1,88,000.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-119","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":119,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Xavier, Yamini and Zoya share profits in the ratio 3:2:1. The deed allows Zoya an annual salary of Rs. 24,000. After the profit for the year had been distributed, it was found that her salary had not been provided. By what amount will Zoya's capital account be credited in the single adjusting entry?","image":"","options":["Rs. 24,000","Rs. 20,000","Rs. 4,000","Rs. 8,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: salary of Rs. 24,000 omitted; ratio 3:2:1.\nHad the salary been allowed, profit would have been Rs. 24,000 lower. That reduction is shared as Xavier Rs. 12,000, Yamini Rs. 8,000 and Zoya Rs. 4,000 (debit).\nZoya's net effect = 24,000 Cr − 4,000 Dr = Rs. 20,000 short.\nThe entry is: Xavier Dr 12,000 and Yamini Dr 8,000, To Zoya 20,000.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-120","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":120,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A partner who earns a commission for himself by using the firm's name and business connections must account for it and pay it to the firm, unless the partners have agreed otherwise.\nStatement II: A partner who secretly runs a business of the same nature as the firm, competing with it, may keep all the profits of that business if he used his own money to run it.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Under the Act, any personal profit a partner earns from a firm transaction, firm property, its business connections or the firm name must be paid to the firm.\nStatement II is incorrect. All profit from a competing business of the same nature must be accounted for and paid to the firm. Using his own money does not change this.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-121","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":121,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Aman and Bhavna share profits in the ratio 3:2. They admit Chirag for a 1/5 share of profits, with a guarantee that his share will not be less than Rs. 30,000. Any deficiency is to be borne by Bhavna alone. Aman and Bhavna continue to share the remaining profit in the ratio 3:2. The firm's profit for the year is Rs. 1,20,000. What is Bhavna's final share of profit?","image":"","options":["Rs. 32,400","Rs. 34,800","Rs. 38,400","Rs. 36,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Chirag gets 1/5. The remaining 4/5 is split 3:2, so Aman gets 12/25 and Bhavna gets 8/25.\nShares: Aman = 1,20,000 × 12/25 = 57,600; Bhavna = 1,20,000 × 8/25 = 38,400; Chirag = 1,20,000 × 1/5 = 24,000.\nDeficiency = 30,000 − 24,000 = 6,000. Bhavna bears all of it because she alone gave the guarantee.\nSo Bhavna's final share = 38,400 − 6,000 = Rs. 32,400.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-122","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":122,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Interest on drawings has already been charged to the partners' capital accounts. Which journal entry transfers this interest to the Profit and Loss Appropriation Account?","image":"","options":["Profit and Loss Appropriation A/c Dr.\nTo Interest on Drawings A/c","Interest on Drawings A/c Dr.\nTo Partners' Capital A/cs","Interest on Drawings A/c Dr.\nTo Profit and Loss Appropriation A/c","Partners' Capital A/cs Dr.\nTo Profit and Loss Appropriation A/c"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Interest on drawings is income for the firm, so it is first charged to partners: Partners' Capital/Current A/cs Dr. To Interest on Drawings A/c. \nThe Interest on Drawings A/c is then closed by transferring its balance to the credit side of the Profit and Loss Appropriation A/c: Interest on Drawings A/c Dr. To P&L Appropriation A/c. \nOption (1) is the pattern used for interest on capital, which is debited to the appropriation account. \nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-123","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":123,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Meher, a partner in a firm, advanced a loan of Rs. 1,50,000 to the firm on October 1, 2019. The partnership deed is silent on interest on loans. The books are closed on March 31 every year. Calculate the interest on Meher's loan for the year ended March 31, 2020.","image":"","options":["Rs. 9,000","Rs. 4,500","Rs. 7,500","Nil"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: loan = Rs. 1,50,000. The deed is silent, so the rate is 6% p.a. The loan was outstanding from Oct 1 to Mar 31, which is 6 months.\nFormula: Interest = Loan × Rate × Time/12.\nSubstituting: 1,50,000 × 6/100 × 6/12 = 4,500.\nSo the interest on the loan is Rs. 4,500. Rs. 9,000 wrongly takes a full year.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-124","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":124,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Neha's capital account showed a balance of Rs. 3,00,000 on April 1, 2023. During the year she made the following changes:\n- July 1, 2023: introduced additional capital of Rs. 60,000\n- January 1, 2024: withdrew Rs. 40,000 from her capital\nInterest on capital is allowed at 10% p.a. Books are closed on March 31. Calculate the interest on Neha's capital for 2023-24.","image":"","options":["Rs. 30,000","Rs. 34,500","Rs. 33,500","Rs. 32,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: the capital balance changes twice, so split the year into periods.\nFormula: Interest = Capital × 10/100 × months/12 for each period.\nSubstituting:\n- April to June: 3,00,000 × 10/100 × 3/12 = Rs. 7,500\n- July to December: 3,60,000 × 10/100 × 6/12 = Rs. 18,000\n- January to March: 3,20,000 × 10/100 × 3/12 = Rs. 8,000\nSo total interest = Rs. 33,500.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-125","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":125,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"The partnership deed of a firm provides that interest on drawings will be charged at 6% p.a. irrespective of the period. Partner Kunal withdrew Rs. 15,000 in total during the year, at different dates. What is the interest on his drawings?","image":"","options":["Rs. 450","Rs. 75","Rs. 900","Rs. 1,800"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: Drawings = Rs. 15,000; rate = 6% flat, irrespective of period.\nKey point: 'Irrespective of the period' means the dates and time are ignored. The rate is applied directly to the total drawings.\nSubstituting: $15{,}000 \\times \\frac{6}{100} = 900$\nRs. 450 wrongly applies a 6-month average period.\nSo interest = Rs. 900.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-126","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":126,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"A, B and C share profits in the ratio 1:2:3. Their fixed capitals are Rs. 3,00,000, Rs. 2,00,000 and Rs. 1,00,000 respectively. After the profits for the year were distributed, it was found that interest on capital had been credited at 10% p.a. instead of the 8% p.a. provided in the deed. Consider the following statements:\n(i) The total excess interest credited to the partners is Rs. 12,000.\n(ii) B's capital account is not affected by the rectification.\n(iii) The single adjusting entry is A's Capital A/c Dr. 4,000 To C's Capital A/c 4,000.\n(iv) Through the Profit and Loss Adjustment Account, the first entry is Profit and Loss Adjustment A/c Dr. 12,000 To A's, B's and C's Capital A/cs.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Excess interest at 2%: A 6,000, B 4,000, C 2,000. Total = Rs. 12,000, to be debited. So (i) is correct.\nReducing the interest raises profit by Rs. 12,000, credited in 1:2:3 as A 2,000, B 4,000, C 6,000.\nNet effect: A Dr. 4,000; B nil; C Cr. 4,000. So (ii) and (iii) are correct.\n(iv) is incorrect. The excess interest is taken back from the partners, so the entry is Partners' Capital A/cs Dr. To P&L Adjustment A/c.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-127","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":127,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Under the product method, the period for each withdrawal is counted from the date of withdrawal to the last day of the accounting year.\nStatement II: Interest under the product method is found as Total of products $\\times$ Rate $\\times \\frac{1}{12}$, because the products are expressed in rupee-months.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Each amount stays withdrawn from its date of withdrawal until the year-end. So the period runs up to the closing date of the books.\nStatement II is correct. Each product is amount × months, so the total is in rupee-months. Multiplying by $\\frac{1}{12}$ converts it into rupee-years, to which the annual rate applies.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-128","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":128,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Kunal and Meher are partners. Kunal withdrew Rs. 25,000 at the end of each quarter during the year ended March 31, 2024. The deed provides for interest on drawings at 8% p.a. Calculate the interest on Kunal's drawings.","image":"","options":["Rs. 3,000","Rs. 5,000","Rs. 4,000","Rs. 2,500"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Rs. 25,000 per quarter, withdrawn at the end of each quarter; rate = 8% p.a.\nTotal drawings = 25,000 × 4 = Rs. 1,00,000.\nAverage period = $\\frac{9+6+3+0}{4} = 4\\frac{1}{2}$ months.\nInterest = $1{,}00{,}000 \\times \\frac{8}{100} \\times \\frac{4.5}{12}$ = Rs. 3,000.\nRs. 5,000 wrongly uses 7½ months, which applies to beginning-of-quarter withdrawals.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-129","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":129,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Arjun and Bela started business on April 1, 2019 with capitals of Rs. 3,00,000 and Rs. 1,00,000. On October 1, 2019 they agreed that each partner's capital should be Rs. 2,00,000. The adjustment was made by withdrawing or introducing cash. Interest on capital is allowed at 8% p.a. What is the interest on Bela's capital for the year ended March 31, 2020?","image":"","options":["Rs. 12,000","Rs. 16,000","Rs. 8,000","Rs. 20,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Bela had Rs. 1,00,000 for 6 months (April–September). From October 1 she had Rs. 2,00,000 for 6 months, after bringing in Rs. 1,00,000.\nFirst period: $1,00,000 \\times \\frac{8}{100} \\times \\frac{6}{12} = 4,000$.\nSecond period: $2,00,000 \\times \\frac{8}{100} \\times \\frac{6}{12} = 8,000$.\nSo interest = Rs. 12,000.\nRs. 16,000 wrongly treats Rs. 2,00,000 as her capital for the whole year.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-130","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":130,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Meera, a partner, withdrew a total of Rs. 48,000 during the year ending March 31, 2024. The dates of withdrawal are not known. The partnership deed provides for interest on drawings at 10% p.a. What is the interest on her drawings?","image":"","options":["Rs. 4,800","Rs. 2,400","Rs. 2,600","Rs. 2,200"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: Total drawings = Rs. 48,000; rate = 10% p.a.; dates not specified.\nRule: Drawings are assumed to be made evenly through the year, so interest is charged for an average period of 6 months.\nSubstituting: $48{,}000 \\times \\frac{10}{100} \\times \\frac{6}{12} = 2{,}400$\nRs. 4,800 wrongly charges interest for a full year.\nSo interest on drawings = Rs. 2,400.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-131","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":131,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Neha's fixed capital is Rs. 2,00,000. Her current account had an opening credit balance of Rs. 10,000 on April 1, 2019. During 2019-20 the firm earned a profit, and the following were recorded for her:\n- Interest on capital: Rs. 10,000\n- Share of profit: Rs. 15,000\n- Drawings: Rs. 60,000\n- Interest on drawings: Rs. 1,500\nWhat is the closing balance of her current account, and where is it shown in the Balance Sheet?","image":"","options":["Rs. 26,500 (Dr.), shown on the assets side","Rs. 6,500 (Dr.), shown on the assets side","Rs. 23,500 (Dr.), shown on the assets side","Rs. 46,500 (Dr.), shown on the assets side"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Credit side = 10,000 (opening) + 10,000 (interest on capital) + 15,000 (share of profit) = Rs. 35,000.\nDebit side = 60,000 (drawings) + 1,500 (interest on drawings) = Rs. 61,500.\nDebits exceed credits by 61,500 − 35,000 = Rs. 26,500, so the balance is Rs. 26,500 (Dr.).\nA debit balance in a current account is shown on the assets side. Her capital of Rs. 2,00,000 stays on the liabilities side.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-132","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":132,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"When the partnership agreement is put in writing, what is the document containing its terms called?","image":"","options":["Memorandum of Association","Partnership Deed","Articles of Association","Prospectus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A written partnership agreement is called the 'Partnership Deed'. It sets out the terms that govern the relationship between the partners.\nThe Memorandum and Articles of Association are documents of a company, not a firm.\nA prospectus is an invitation by a company to the public to buy its securities.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-133","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":133,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Errors such as omission of a partner's commission, discovered after profits have been distributed, can be corrected without altering the old accounts.\nReason (R): Such errors can be rectified either through a Profit and Loss Adjustment Account or by passing an adjusting entry directly in the partners' capital accounts.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that, instead of altering old accounts, the necessary adjustments are made later.\nR is true. It names the two methods: the Profit and Loss Adjustment Account and direct adjustment in capital accounts.\nR explains how the correction is possible without reopening old accounts, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-134","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":134,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Amar and Bhanu share profits in the ratio 2 : 1. They admit Chirag for a $\\frac{1}{10}$ share in profits, with a guaranteed minimum of Rs. 30,000. Amar and Bhanu continue to share the remaining profit 2 : 1, but agree to bear any deficiency on account of the guarantee in the ratio 3 : 2. The firm's profit for the year is Rs. 2,00,000. What amount of profit will finally be credited to Bhanu?","image":"","options":["Rs. 56,667","Rs. 60,000","Rs. 54,000","Rs. 56,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"New shares: Amar = $\\frac{9}{10}\\times\\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{6}{10}$, Bhanu = $\\frac{3}{10}$, Chirag = $\\frac{1}{10}$, giving the ratio 6:3:1.\nShares of profit: Amar 1,20,000, Bhanu 60,000, Chirag 20,000.\nDeficiency = 30,000 − 20,000 = 10,000, borne in the agreed ratio 3:2. Amar bears 6,000 and Bhanu bears 4,000.\nBhanu gets 60,000 − 4,000 = Rs. 56,000.\nRs. 56,667 wrongly uses the ratio 2:1 for the deficiency.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-135","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":135,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Kiran and Lata are partners. The firm earned a profit of Rs. 40,000 before considering two items:\n- Kiran's loan of Rs. 60,000, given at the start of the year with no agreement on interest\n- the manager's commission of Rs. 4,000\n\nWhat amount of net profit will be transferred to the Profit and Loss Appropriation Account?","image":"","options":["Rs. 30,000","Rs. 36,000","Rs. 32,400","Rs. 36,400"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: profit Rs. 40,000; loan Rs. 60,000 with no agreed rate; manager's commission Rs. 4,000. \nWith no agreement, the Partnership Act allows interest on a partner's loan at 6% p.a.: 6% × 60,000 = Rs. 3,600. \nInterest on the loan and the manager's commission are both charges, deducted in the P&L A/c. \nNet profit = 40,000 − 3,600 − 4,000 = Rs. 32,400. \nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-136","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":136,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: If the partnership deed has no express agreement on a matter, the provisions of the Indian Partnership Act, 1932 apply.\nStatement II: Once a partnership deed is registered with the Registrar of Firms, its clauses cannot be altered even with the consent of all partners.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: NCERT states that the Act's provisions apply wherever the deed is silent.\nStatement II is incorrect: the clauses can be altered with the consent of all partners.\nRegistration does not freeze the terms of the deed.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-137","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":137,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Ravi withdrew Rs. 20,000 at the end of each quarter during the year 2023-24. Interest on drawings is charged at 6% p.a. Calculate the interest on his drawings.","image":"","options":["Rs. 3,000","Rs. 1,800","Rs. 2,400","Rs. 2,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: Rs. 20,000 per quarter at the end of each quarter, rate 6% p.a.\nTotal drawings = 20,000 × 4 = Rs. 80,000.\nAverage period = (9 + 0)/2 = 4.5 months.\nInterest = 80,000 × 6/100 × 4.5/12 = Rs. 1,800.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-138","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":138,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (situation) with List-II (period for which interest on that drawing is charged):\nList-I\n(A) Drawing on April 1, books closed on March 31\n(B) Total drawings given, dates not specified\n(C) Drawing on October 31, books closed on March 31\n(D) Drawing on September 30, books closed on December 31\nList-II\n(i) 3 months\n(ii) 5 months\n(iii) 6 months\n(iv) 12 months","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) April 1 to March 31 is the whole year, so 12 months: (iv).\n(B) When dates are unknown, an average of 6 months is assumed: (iii).\n(C) October 31 to March 31 covers November to March, so 5 months: (ii).\n(D) September 30 to December 31 is 3 months: (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-139","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":139,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Kavya is a partner in a firm whose books are closed on March 31 every year. During 2023-24 she withdrew Rs. 48,000 in total for personal use. The exact dates of withdrawal are not known. The partnership deed provides for interest on drawings @ 10% p.a. What amount of interest on drawings will be charged to Kavya?","image":"","options":["Rs. 2,400","Rs. 4,800","Rs. 2,200","Rs. 2,600"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Total drawings = Rs. 48,000, rate = 10% p.a., dates not specified.\nRule: When dates are not given, assume the amount was withdrawn evenly through the year. Interest is then charged for an average period of 6 months.\nFormula: Interest = Drawings × Rate × 6/12\nSubstituting: 48,000 × 10/100 × 6/12 = Rs. 2,400\nRs. 4,800 is wrong because it charges interest for the full 12 months. Rs. 2,200 and Rs. 2,600 use 5½ and 6½ months, which apply only to fixed monthly withdrawals made at the end or beginning of each month.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-140","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":140,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Read the following statements about the nature of a partnership firm:\n(i) An oral agreement between partners is as valid as a written one.\n(ii) A partnership firm is a separate legal entity, distinct from its partners.\n(iii) The Companies Act 2013 sets a ceiling of 100 partners in a firm, and the Central Government has prescribed a maximum of 50.\n(iv) Five friends who jointly run a free tuition centre for poor children form a partnership.\n(v) A partner's private assets can be used to pay off the debts of the firm.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the Act does not require a written agreement. A written deed is only preferred, to avoid disputes.\n(ii) Incorrect: a partnership firm has no separate legal entity apart from its partners.\n(iii) Correct: under Section 464 of the Companies Act 2013 the limit cannot exceed 100, and the Central Government has prescribed 50.\n(iv) Incorrect: a charitable activity involves no sharing of profits, so it is not a partnership.\n(v) Correct: the liability of partners is unlimited.\nSo three statements, (i), (iii) and (v), are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-141","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":141,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"A firm maintains partners' capital accounts under the fixed capital method. How many of the following items will be recorded in a partner's Current Account?\n(i) Drawings\n(ii) Interest on capital\n(iii) Additional capital introduced\n(iv) Share of profit\n(v) Permanent withdrawal of capital\n(vi) Salary to partner","image":"","options":["3","4","5","2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Under the fixed capital method, the capital account records only fresh capital introduced and permanent withdrawal of capital.\n(iii) and (v) therefore go to the Capital Account.\nEvery other adjustment goes to the Current Account. These are (i) drawings, (ii) interest on capital, (iv) share of profit and (vi) salary.\nSo 4 items are recorded in the Current Account.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-142","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":142,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements regarding interest on drawings is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Under the product method, each amount withdrawn is multiplied by the number of months from its withdrawal to the end of the accounting year.","When interest is charged at a flat rate irrespective of the period, the dates of withdrawal do not affect the interest.","An amount withdrawn on the last day of the accounting year attracts interest for one month.","The product method is used when partners withdraw different amounts at different intervals."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: this is how the product method works, as in the Shahnaz illustration.\n(2) Correct: a flat rate is applied to the total drawings regardless of time.\n(3) Incorrect: a withdrawal on the closing day stays outstanding for 0 months, so it bears no interest. In the NCERT quarterly example, the March 31 drawing is taken for 0 months.\n(4) Correct: NCERT prescribes the product method for varying amounts and dates.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-143","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":143,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A firm whose deed provides interest on capital at 8% p.a. incurs a net loss of Rs. 40,000. No interest on capital is credited to the partners for that year.\nReason (R): Interest on capital is an appropriation of profit, so it is allowed only when the firm has earned a profit.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. When the firm suffers a loss, no interest on capital is allowed, even if the deed provides for it. The loss is simply shared in the profit sharing ratio.\nR is true. Interest on capital is debited to the Profit and Loss Appropriation Account, so it can be paid only out of profits.\nBecause interest on capital is an appropriation of profit, there is nothing to appropriate in a loss year. R therefore correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-144","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":144,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Arnav and Bela are partners. Their partnership deed says nothing about interest on loans. Arnav advances money to the firm for business purposes. At what rate is Arnav entitled to interest on this loan under the Indian Partnership Act, 1932?","image":"","options":["No interest, because the deed is silent","6% per annum","10% per annum","12% per annum"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: when the deed is silent, the Indian Partnership Act, 1932 allows a partner interest on a loan given to the firm at 6% p.a.\nThis right exists even when the deed is silent, so option (1) is wrong.\nThe Act does not mention 10% or 12%. A partner may claim these rates only if the partners have agreed to them.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-145","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":145,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Five persons who join hands to run a free coaching centre for poor children, with no intention of earning profit, constitute a partnership.\nStatement II: Although the Section 4 definition mentions only sharing of profits, sharing of losses is implied in a partnership.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. A partnership requires an agreement to share the profits and losses of a business. Persons joining hands for a charitable activity are not partners.\nStatement II is correct. NCERT notes that the definition speaks only of profits, but sharing of losses is implied.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-146","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":146,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about methods of maintaining partners' capital accounts is correct?","image":"","options":["Under the fixed capital method, the partner's current account always shows a credit balance.","Under the fluctuating capital method, two accounts, capital and current, are maintained for each partner.","In the absence of any instruction, capital accounts are prepared under the fixed capital method.","Under the fluctuating capital method, a partner's capital account may sometimes show a debit balance."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: under the fixed capital method, the current account may show a debit or a credit balance.\n(2) Incorrect: the fluctuating method keeps only one account (the capital account) for each partner.\n(3) Incorrect: when no instruction is given, the fluctuating capital method is followed.\n(4) Correct: losses and drawings are recorded directly in the capital account, so its balance can turn debit.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-147","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":147,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about partnership are correct?\n(i) A partnership firm has a separate legal entity apart from its partners.\n(ii) A firm must have at least two partners.\n(iii) Under the Companies Act 2013, the maximum number of partners that may be prescribed cannot exceed 100.\n(iv) A partnership agreement is valid only when it is in writing.\n(v) A partner's private assets can be used to pay off the firm's debts.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Incorrect: a firm has no separate legal entity apart from its partners.\n(ii) Correct: the minimum number of partners is two.\n(iii) Correct: under Section 464 the prescribed number cannot exceed 100.\n(iv) Incorrect: an oral agreement is equally valid.\n(v) Correct: liability is unlimited, so private assets can be used.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-148","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":148,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"The partnership deed of a firm is silent on the following matters. Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Profit sharing ratio\n(B) Interest on capital\n(C) Interest on drawings\n(D) Interest on partner's loan\nList-II\n(i) Shared equally\n(ii) 6% per annum\n(iii) Not allowed\n(iv) Not charged","image":"","options":["A-(i), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(iii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"When the deed is silent, the provisions of the Partnership Act apply:\n(A) Profits and losses are shared equally, irrespective of capital, so A-(i).\n(B) Interest on capital is not allowed, so B-(iii).\n(C) Interest on drawings is not charged, so C-(iv).\n(D) Interest on a partner's loan is given at 6% p.a., so D-(ii).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-149","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":149,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, how should a partnership deed be prepared and registered?","image":"","options":["Prepared under the Companies Act, 2013 and registered with the Registrar of Companies","Prepared under the Stamp Act and preferably registered with the Registrar of Firms","Prepared under the Income Tax Act and registered with the Reserve Bank of India","Prepared under the Stamp Act and compulsorily registered with SEBI"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the deed should be properly drafted as per the provisions of the Stamp Act.\nIt should preferably be registered with the Registrar of Firms. Registration is described as desirable, not compulsory.\nThe Registrar of Companies, the RBI and SEBI deal with companies or the financial markets, not with partnership deeds.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-150","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":150,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"X and Y share profits in the ratio 3:2. They admit Z for a 1/5 share of profits, and X and Y continue to share the remaining profit in 3:2. X alone guarantees that Z's share will be at least Rs. 30,000. The firm's profit for the year is Rs. 1,20,000. What amount of profit will finally be credited to X?","image":"","options":["Rs. 54,000","Rs. 51,600","Rs. 57,600","Rs. 50,400"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: Z's share is 1/5. The remaining 4/5 is shared by X and Y in 3:2, so X gets 12/25 and Y gets 8/25.\nSubstituting: X's share = 1,20,000 × 12/25 = 57,600. Z's share = 1,20,000 × 1/5 = 24,000.\nZ's deficiency = 30,000 − 24,000 = 6,000, borne by X alone.\nSo X gets 57,600 − 6,000 = Rs. 51,600. Rs. 54,000 would apply only if X and Y shared the deficiency in 3:2.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-151","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":151,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"A partner withdraws a fixed amount on the last day of every month for 12 months. Using the average period formula, (months for first drawing + months for last drawing)/2, for how many months is interest charged on the total drawings?","image":"","options":["6.5 months","6 months","5.5 months","4.5 months"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"If the first drawing is made at the end of the first month, it stays outstanding for 11 months.\nThe last drawing is made on the last day of the year, so it is outstanding for 0 months.\nAverage period = (11 + 0)/2 = 5.5 months.\nThe most tempting wrong option, 6.5 months, applies to drawings made at the beginning of each month: (12 + 1)/2.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-152","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":152,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Ravi and Sonal share profits in the ratio 2:3. Their capitals are Rs. 2,00,000 and Rs. 3,00,000. The deed provides interest on capital at 10% p.a. The firm's profit for the year is only Rs. 30,000. How much will be credited to Ravi's capital account as interest on capital?","image":"","options":["Rs. 20,000","Rs. 15,000","Rs. 18,000","Rs. 12,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: interest due is Rs. 20,000 to Ravi and Rs. 30,000 to Sonal, a total of Rs. 50,000. The profit is only Rs. 30,000.\nRule: interest is restricted to the available profit, which is shared in the ratio of interest due, 20,000 : 30,000 = 2:3.\nSubstituting: Ravi = 30,000 × 2/5 = Rs. 12,000.\nSo Ravi is credited with Rs. 12,000. Option (1) ignores the restriction to available profit.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-153","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":153,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"P, Q and R are partners sharing profits in the ratio 2:2:1. Their capitals are Rs. 3,00,000, Rs. 2,00,000 and Rs. 1,00,000. Interest on capital is allowed at 10% p.a. R is guaranteed a profit of Rs. 30,000, excluding interest on capital, and any deficiency is borne by P. The profit before interest on capital is Rs. 1,80,000. What is the total amount credited to R's capital account (interest on capital plus share of profit)?","image":"","options":["Rs. 30,000","Rs. 34,000","Rs. 36,000","Rs. 40,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: profit before interest is Rs. 1,80,000 and interest is 10% on capitals.\nInterest on capital: P = 30,000, Q = 20,000, R = 10,000. Total = Rs. 60,000.\nDivisible profit = 1,80,000 − 60,000 = Rs. 1,20,000. R's share = 1/5 × 1,20,000 = Rs. 24,000.\nThe guarantee excludes interest, so the deficiency = 30,000 − 24,000 = Rs. 6,000, borne by P.\nTotal credited to R = 10,000 + 30,000 = Rs. 40,000.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-154","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":154,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Arjun withdraws Rs. 3,000 every month. Interest on drawings is charged at 8% p.a. By how much would the interest on his drawings for the year be higher if he withdrew at the beginning of every month instead of at the end of every month?","image":"","options":["Rs. 120","Rs. 1,560","Rs. 240","Rs. 360"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: total drawings = 3,000 × 12 = Rs. 36,000, rate 8% p.a.\nAt the beginning of each month: 36,000 × 8/100 × 6.5/12 = Rs. 1,560.\nAt the end of each month: 36,000 × 8/100 × 5.5/12 = Rs. 1,320.\nDifference = 1,560 − 1,320 = Rs. 240. This equals interest on Rs. 36,000 for 1 month.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-155","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":155,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Dev and Isha are partners in a firm that closes its books on December 31 every year. During 2023, Dev made the following drawings:\nFebruary 1, 2023: Rs. 10,000\nMay 31, 2023: Rs. 6,000\nSeptember 30, 2023: Rs. 9,000\nNovember 30, 2023: Rs. 12,000\nInterest on drawings is charged @ 12% p.a. Using the product method, what is the interest on Dev's drawings for the year ending December 31, 2023?","image":"","options":["Rs. 1,910","Rs. 3,020","Rs. 2,220","Rs. 1,810"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1: Count the months from each withdrawal to the year-end, December 31 (not March 31).\nFeb 1: 11 months, so the product is 10,000 × 11 = 1,10,000.\nMay 31: 7 months, so the product is 6,000 × 7 = 42,000.\nSep 30: 3 months, so the product is 9,000 × 3 = 27,000.\nNov 30: 1 month, so the product is 12,000 × 1 = 12,000.\nStep 2: Total of products = 1,91,000.\nStep 3: Interest = 1,91,000 × 12/100 × 1/12 = Rs. 1,910.\nWhy the others fail: Rs. 3,020 counts the months up to March 31. Rs. 2,220 wrongly applies an average of 6 months to the total of Rs. 37,000.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-156","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":156,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Satvik, a partner, withdrew Rs. 20,000 on the first day of every quarter during the year ending March 31, 2020 (April 1, July 1, October 1 and January 1). The deed provides interest on drawings at 9% p.a. Calculate the interest on Satvik's drawings.","image":"","options":["Rs. 4,500","Rs. 2,700","Rs. 3,600","Rs. 7,200"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Total drawings = $4 \\times 20,000$ = Rs. 80,000, withdrawn at the beginning of each quarter. Rate = 9% p.a.\nAverage period = $\\frac{12+9+6+3}{4}$ = 7.5 months.\nSubstituting: $80,000 \\times \\frac{9}{100} \\times \\frac{7.5}{12}$ = Rs. 4,500.\nRs. 2,700 uses 4.5 months, which applies to end-of-quarter drawings.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-157","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":157,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"A partner withdrew an equal amount on the first day of every month throughout the year. The interest on drawings charged at 9% p.a. amounted to Rs. 1,170. What was the amount withdrawn each month?","image":"","options":["Rs. 2,000","Rs. 2,167","Rs. 1,800","Rs. 2,400"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: interest = Rs. 1,170, rate 9% p.a. Drawings at the beginning of each month give an average period of 6.5 months.\nFormula: Interest = Total drawings × 9/100 × 6.5/12.\nSubstituting: 1,170 = D × 0.09 × 6.5/12, so D = 1,170 × 12 / 0.585 = Rs. 24,000.\nMonthly drawing = 24,000/12 = Rs. 2,000.\nUsing 6 months instead of 6.5 would wrongly give about Rs. 2,167.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-158","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":158,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Kabir is a partner whose fixed capital is ₹4,00,000. His current account showed a credit balance of ₹50,000 on April 1, 2023. For the year 2023-24, the following details are available:\nInterest on capital ₹24,000\nSalary ₹30,000\nShare of profit ₹40,000\nDrawings ₹60,000\nInterest on drawings ₹1,800\nWhat is the closing balance of Kabir's current account on March 31, 2024?","image":"","options":["₹82,200 (Cr.)","₹4,82,200 (Cr.)","₹85,800 (Cr.)","₹32,200 (Cr.)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Opening current balance ₹50,000 (Cr.). Capital is fixed, so it does not enter the current account.\nCredits: 50,000 + 24,000 + 30,000 + 40,000 = ₹1,44,000.\nDebits: drawings 60,000 + interest on drawings 1,800 = ₹61,800.\nClosing balance = 1,44,000 − 61,800 = ₹82,200 (Cr.).\nOption (2) wrongly adds the fixed capital. Option (3) wrongly credits interest on drawings.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-159","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":159,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: If the firm's profit is less than the total interest on capital due to partners, the profit is distributed among them in the ratio of their interest on capital.\nStatement II: If the firm incurs a net loss, interest on capital is still credited to partners, and the increased loss is shared in the profit sharing ratio.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Interest is restricted to the available profit, so the profit is effectively shared in the ratio of interest on capital. For example, interest of Rs. 12,000 and Rs. 16,000 against a profit of Rs. 14,000 gives Rs. 6,000 and Rs. 8,000 (ratio 3:4).\nStatement II is incorrect. When the firm suffers a loss, no interest on capital, salary or remuneration is allowed. Only the loss itself is shared in the profit sharing ratio.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-160","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":160,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Neha and Arjun maintain their capital accounts under the fixed capital method. Which one of the following transactions will be recorded in Neha's capital account rather than in her current account?","image":"","options":["Interest on capital allowed to Neha","Neha's share of profit for the year","Fresh capital of ₹1,00,000 brought in by Neha as per the agreement","Drawings of ₹5,000 per month made by Neha"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Under the fixed capital method, the capital account changes only in two cases: when additional capital is introduced or when capital is permanently withdrawn.\nAll other items go to the current account. These include interest on capital, share of profit or loss, drawings, interest on drawings, salary and commission.\nFresh capital brought in is therefore credited to Neha's capital account.\nDrawings can look like a reduction of capital, but regular drawings are debited to the current account. They are not a permanent withdrawal of capital.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-161","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":161,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Read the following statements about guarantee of minimum profit to a partner.\nStatement I: If only one of the old partners gives the guarantee to the new partner, the whole deficiency is borne by that partner alone.\nStatement II: In such a case, the shares of profit of the other old partners are not reduced on account of the guarantee.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. When the guarantee is given by one old partner individually, the full shortfall is charged to that partner only.\nStatement II is correct. The partners who did not give the guarantee bear none of the deficiency, so their shares stay as per the profit sharing ratio.\nExample from NCERT: if only Rakshita guarantees Kanishka, Madhulika still gets her full Rs. 40,000, while Rakshita's share falls to Rs. 55,000.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-162","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":162,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (Item) with List-II (Accounting treatment):\nList-I\n(A) Interest on drawings\n(B) Partner's salary\n(C) Interest on partner's loan\n(D) Share of loss after appropriations\nList-II\n(i) Debited to Profit and Loss A/c as a charge\n(ii) Credited to P&L Appropriation A/c; partner's capital/current A/c is debited\n(iii) Debited to P&L Appropriation A/c; partner's capital/current A/c is credited\n(iv) Partners' capital/current A/cs debited; P&L Appropriation A/c credited","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A-(ii): Interest on drawings is charged to partners (debit to their capital/current A/cs) and is a gain credited to the P&L Appropriation A/c.\nB-(iii): Partner's salary is an appropriation. It is debited to the P&L Appropriation A/c and credited to the partner's capital/current A/c.\nC-(i): Interest on a partner's loan is a charge against profit, debited to the P&L A/c.\nD-(iv): A loss is distributed by debiting partners' capital/current A/cs and crediting the P&L Appropriation A/c.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-163","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":163,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Kiran and Leela started a firm without any written or oral agreement on financial matters. Kiran contributed Rs. 6,00,000 and Leela Rs. 2,00,000 as capital. Which of the following statements is correct as per the Indian Partnership Act, 1932?","image":"","options":["Profits will be shared in the ratio 3 : 1, the ratio of their capitals","Interest on capital will be allowed at 6% p.a.","No interest on capital will be allowed, and profits will be shared equally","Interest on drawings will be charged at 6% p.a."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: when the deed is silent, profits are shared equally, irrespective of capital.\n(2) Incorrect: no interest on capital is allowed without an express agreement. The 6% rate applies only to a partner's loan.\n(3) Correct: there is no interest on capital, and profits are shared equally.\n(4) Incorrect: no interest is charged on drawings if the deed is silent.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-164","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":164,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Tanvi manages the day-to-day work of the firm full-time while her partner Dev works only occasionally. Since the partnership deed is silent on remuneration, Tanvi cannot claim a salary from the firm.\nReason (R): The Indian Partnership Act 1932 provides that no partner is entitled to salary or other remuneration for taking part in the conduct of the business unless the deed provides for it.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Working harder than the other partner does not create a right to salary. Remuneration must be provided for in the deed.\nR is true. This is exactly the provision of the Act that applies when the deed is silent.\nR is the rule that makes A true, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-165","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":165,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Case: The opening capital of Meera, a partner, is not available. Her capital account showed a closing balance of Rs. 1,90,000 on March 31, 2024. The following entries had already been recorded in her capital account during the year:\n- Drawings of Rs. 20,000 were debited\n- Her share of profit, Rs. 30,000, was credited\n- Additional capital of Rs. 60,000 introduced on January 1, 2024 was credited\nInterest on capital is to be allowed at 12% p.a. What is the interest on Meera's capital for 2023-24?","image":"","options":["Rs. 22,800","Rs. 16,200","Rs. 14,400","Rs. 21,600"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Step 1: Work back to the opening capital.\nOpening capital = Closing capital + Drawings − Profit credited − Additional capital\n= 1,90,000 + 20,000 − 30,000 − 60,000 = Rs. 1,20,000.\nStep 2: Interest on opening capital for 12 months = 1,20,000 × 12/100 = Rs. 14,400.\nStep 3: Interest on additional capital for 3 months (January to March) = 60,000 × 12/100 × 3/12 = Rs. 1,800.\nSo total interest = Rs. 16,200.\nOption (1) wrongly uses the closing capital.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-166","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":166,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Only the closing capitals of the partners are given. Interest on capital must be calculated on the opening capital.\nStatement I: Drawings made during the year, already debited to the capital account, are added to the closing capital to work back the opening capital.\nStatement II: The share of profit already credited to the capital account is added to the closing capital to work back the opening capital.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Drawings reduced the capital during the year, so they are added back to reach the opening balance.\nStatement II is incorrect. The profit credited increased the capital, so it must be deducted, not added.\nAdditional capital brought in during the year is also deducted.\nThis is the method used in the Josh–Krish case.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-167","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":167,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"A partner withdraws a fixed amount on the first day of every quarter during an accounting year. The partnership deed provides for interest on drawings. For what period is interest calculated on the total amount withdrawn during the year?","image":"","options":["4½ months","6 months","6½ months","7½ months"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The four quarterly drawings remain with the partner for 12, 9, 6 and 3 months.\nAverage period = $\\frac{12+9+6+3}{4} = 7\\frac{1}{2}$ months. The short formula gives the same result: $\\frac{12+3}{2}$.\n4½ months applies to withdrawals at the END of each quarter, $\\frac{9+0}{2}$.\n6½ months applies to monthly withdrawals at the beginning of each month.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-168","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":168,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Kabir and Leena are partners with capitals of Rs. 5,00,000 and Rs. 2,00,000. Their partnership deed says nothing about interest on capital. The firm earned a profit of Rs. 1,40,000 for the year. Kabir demands interest on capital at 6% p.a. Which treatment is correct?","image":"","options":["Kabir gets interest at 6% p.a. because that is the rate fixed by the Partnership Act","No interest on capital is allowed; the whole profit is shared equally","Interest at 6% p.a. is allowed to both partners because the firm earned a profit","No interest is allowed; the profit is shared in the capital ratio 5:2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Under the Indian Partnership Act 1932, a partner cannot claim interest on capital as a matter of right. It is allowed only when the deed expressly provides for it.\nThe deed is silent here, so no interest on capital is allowed.\nWhen the deed is silent, profits are also shared equally, whatever the capital contributed. So the profit is shared equally, not 5:2.\nThe 6% p.a. rate under the Act applies only to a partner's loan to the firm, not to capital.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-169","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":169,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the form of a partnership agreement is correct?","image":"","options":["A partnership agreement is valid only if it is written and registered.","An oral partnership agreement is valid, but a written agreement is preferred to avoid disputes.","An oral partnership agreement is valid only if the firm has fewer than 10 partners.","A written agreement is compulsory only when partners contribute unequal capital."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The Indian Partnership Act, 1932 does not require the agreement to be in writing, so an oral agreement is equally valid.\nA written deed is preferred because it reduces the chance of disputes.\nOptions (1), (3) and (4) add conditions that the Act does not impose.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-170","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":170,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Asha, Bina and Charu share profits in the ratio 3 : 2 : 1. Charu is guaranteed a minimum profit of Rs. 20,000 a year. The firm's net profit for the year is Rs. 90,000. What is the deficiency that will have to be made good to Charu?","image":"","options":["Rs. 15,000","Rs. 5,000","Rs. 20,000","Rs. 3,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: profit Rs. 90,000, ratio 3:2:1, guarantee to Charu Rs. 20,000.\nFormula: Deficiency = Guaranteed amount − Share as per profit sharing ratio.\nCharu's share = $\\frac{1}{6}$ × 90,000 = Rs. 15,000.\nDeficiency = 20,000 − 15,000 = Rs. 5,000.\nRs. 15,000 is only her share under the ratio, not the shortfall.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-171","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":171,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Commission payable to the firm's manager is debited to the Profit and Loss Appropriation Account because it is calculated as a percentage of profit.\nStatement II: Rent paid to a partner for the use of her personal building as the firm's godown is a charge against profit and is debited to the Profit and Loss Account.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. The manager is an employee, not a partner, so his commission is a business expense. In the Amitabh–Babul illustration, the manager's commission is shown in the P&L A/c. Calculating it as a percentage of profit does not make it an appropriation.\nStatement II is correct. NCERT notes that rent paid to a partner for her building is an expense, so it is charged against profit in the P&L A/c.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-PARTNERSHIP-BASICS-172","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","qno":172,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","type":"mcq","question":"Under the fixed capital method, how many of the following items are recorded on the CREDIT side of a partner's current account?\nSalary to partner; Commission to partner; Interest on capital; Interest on drawings; Drawings; Share of profit; Share of loss","image":"","options":["3","4","5","2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Credit side (amounts due to the partner): salary, commission, interest on capital and share of profit. That makes 4 items.\nDebit side (amounts charged to the partner): interest on drawings, drawings and share of loss.\nA common mistake is to place interest on drawings on the credit side. It is income for the firm but a charge on the partner.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Accounting for Partnership: Basic Concepts","difficulty":"medium"}]});
